var e={notice:`The data here is/may be copyrighted and NOT included in the GPLv3 license. Converted from MegaMek's unit files (https://github.com/MegaMek/megamek): MegaMek Data (C) 2025-2026 by The MegaMek Team, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).`,shared:[{engine:`InterComBust 180 ICE`,armor:`BAR 6`},{$k:[`tag`,`parameter`],$v:[[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[`quirk-fast-reload`,null],[`quirk-obsolete`,2580],[`quirk-narrow-low-profile`,null],[`quirk-poor-performance`,null]]},{front:22,left:22,right:22,rear:12,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`],{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`fusion`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:1,chassisModifications:[],bar:6,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`advanced`},{manufacturer:`Rebel Industrial Technologies Inc.`,primaryFactory:`Mars`,targeting:`Advanced Fire Control`},[`small-laser`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`halfTon`,`feedsWeaponTag`],`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,[`quirk-hard-to-pilot`,null],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`,null],[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`lrm-5`,`turret`,`#2`,null],[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`],{manufacturer:`Pacific-Euro Consolidated Aerospace Systems`,primaryFactory:`Terra`},`The Cobra VTOL Transport traces its origins to the formative years of the Terran Hegemony's military organization, commissioned from Pacific-Euro Consolidated Aerospace Systems on Terra in 2317. The design emerged from Director-General James McKenna's sweeping initiative to replace the fragmented procurement practices of the Alliance Global Militia with a standardized, purpose-built military equipment base. McKenna's vision demanded a light transport capable of operating across the full range of Hegemony operations, from battlefield resupply runs to rear-area logistics, and the tilt-rotor Cobra answered that specification with a design that proved both practical and adaptable. Capable of delivering troops, supplies, and light vehicles to contested forward positions and evacuating casualties on the return, the Cobra defined Hegemony military transport operations for most of the twenty-fourth century and established the tilt-rotor transport template that would echo through centuries of successor designs.`,`The Cobra VTOL Transport uses an internal combustion engine driving a tilt-rotor assembly for propulsion, achieving a cruise speed of approximately 130 kilometers per hour. While this performance lagged behind faster VTOL designs of the period, the Cobra's strength lay in its carrying capacity rather than raw speed. The spacious internal hold accommodates nine tons of cargo and is fitted with three access doors, right, left, and rear, allowing rapid loading and offloading in the field. The cargo bay accepts interchangeable kit packages, allowing units to reconfigure the vehicle for troop transport, medical evacuation, reconnaissance, airborne command post, or VIP duties without dedicated maintenance facilities.

The vehicle also supports a slung load beneath the airframe, extending its utility to delivery of light artillery pieces, small vehicles, or supplemental bulk cargo alongside its internal carry. For self-defense, four machine guns cover multiple arcs and are supported by an advanced fire control system to improve crew targeting effectiveness under operational conditions. BAR-6 composite armor provides adequate protection for a transport operating in contested airspace, though the Cobra relies primarily on speed and escort for survivability in active combat environments. A three-person crew handles flight, vehicle operations, and gunnery during missions.`,`The Cobra VTOL Transport's development came at a defining moment in Terran Hegemony history. Director-General James McKenna's rise to power inaugurated a new era of military standardization across what had been the loosely organized Alliance Global Militia. Prior to McKenna's reforms, equipment procurement had been inconsistent at best, with contracts regularly awarded for designs already rejected by the military hierarchy. The Cobra was among the earliest products of the new mandate requiring uniform standards, with Pacific-Euro Consolidated Aerospace Systems receiving the production contract and beginning manufacture in 2317.

The design proved an immediate success and remained the standard Hegemony VTOL transport for most of the twenty-fourth century. Field conversion kits developed in parallel with the original model allowed individual Cobras to be rapidly reconfigured for medical evacuation, airborne command posts, VIP transport, and reconnaissance missions without factory support. The design's adaptability encouraged a continuous stream of specialized configurations, and hundreds of examples entered service across all branches of Hegemony forces. Many decommissioned Cobras eventually found their way into civilian hands, serving government agencies, commercial operators, and the civilian aftermarket as workhorses for nearly every conceivable transport purpose.

By the latter decades of the twenty-fourth century, advancing aerospace technology and the introduction of more capable designs had begun displacing the original Cobra in front-line service. The final military production orders, fulfilled in 2489, incorporated a fusion power plant that improved performance margins and extended the design's useful service life before the original line was discontinued entirely. The Cobra VTOL Transport did not survive to witness the founding of the Star League, but its influence persisted in Hegemony operational records and engineering documentation that directly informed Aldis Industries' development of the Star League-era Cobra Transport VTOL. The tilt-rotor transport concept the Cobra pioneered would prove remarkably enduring, with echoes of its basic design philosophy visible in transport VTOLs produced centuries later across the Inner Sphere.`,{$k:[`tag`,`parameter`],$v:[[`quirk-vtol-rotor-arrangement`,`dual`],[`quirk-obsolete`,2470],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`,null]]},{front:10,left:8,right:8,rear:6,turret:0,rotor:2,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`halfTon`,`feedsWeaponTag`],$v:[[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#1`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`right`,`#2`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`left`,`#3`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`rear`,`#4`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#5`,!0,`machine-gun`]]},{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`ice`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:0,chassisModifications:[],bar:6,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`advanced`},`The Generic Expandable Services Vehicle, commonly known as the GESV, is a multipurpose wheeled support platform developed by the Federated Suns and introduced in 3066. Built around OmniVehicle technology, the design can be rapidly reconfigured to serve virtually any rear-echelon role, from mobile command post to field kitchen to frontline medical facility. The platform serves military units, government agencies, police forces, and civilian relief organizations operating in field environments. Its purpose-built trailer extends the tractor's capabilities further, offering additional configurations for personnel housing and cargo transport. The GESV's design philosophy emphasizes adaptability over specialization, allowing any organization engaged in extended field operations to meet diverse support needs with a single standardized platform rather than a fleet of dedicated vehicles. This flexibility, combined with straightforward maintenance requirements, has made the design a common sight in Federated Suns rear-echelon formations.`,`The GESV tractor is a medium wheeled support vehicle powered by a fuel cell engine. The fuel cell system eliminates dependence on volatile liquid fuels, reducing the logistical burden in forward areas and improving fire safety when the vehicle is configured as a medical unit or command post. The engine provides sufficient speed to keep pace with most mechanized formations, and the vehicle carries enough fuel for a range of approximately 2,700 kilometers.

Two tons of commercial-grade armor rated at BAR 7 protect the crew and internal equipment against light weapons fire and battlefield debris. This protection level is adequate for a support platform operating behind the front lines, though it provides little security against direct combat engagement. The GESV carries no integral weapons, leaving defense entirely to escort forces or crew personal arms, which is a meaningful limitation in fluid operational environments.

As an OmniVehicle, the GESV dedicates eleven tons of pod space to mission equipment, enabling installation of any combination of modules within that weight limit. A crew of two operates the vehicle itself, with configuration-specific personnel added as required. The tractor can pull up to two purpose-built trailers simultaneously, substantially multiplying available support capacity without additional prime movers. The trade-off for this flexibility is entirely in the vehicle's lack of self-defense capability.`,`The GESV emerged from a Federated Suns initiative to standardize rear-echelon support infrastructure. By the mid-3060s, the complexities of operating across multiple theaters had exposed the limitations of specialized support vehicles. Different organizations fielded incompatible equipment, creating maintenance and logistics complications that impaired operational efficiency across extended campaigns.

The OmniVehicle architecture provided the engineering solution. By applying the same modular technology used in OmniMechs to a wheeled support platform, engineers produced a vehicle that could be refitted between missions to serve whichever role was most needed. The tractor's eleven tons of pod space accommodated a command post module, a field kitchen, or a MASH unit, while the companion trailer expanded capacity for bunking, cargo, or workspace needs.

Introduced in 3066, the GESV entered service during the final years of the FedCom Civil War. Its timing proved fortunate, as the conflict generated exactly the kind of extended, multi-role operational demand the design was built to address. AFFS rear-echelon units quickly adopted the platform, and its reputation for versatility spread beyond the Federated Suns. Civilian agencies, police departments, and non-governmental relief organizations operating in conflict zones began acquiring the vehicle as well.

The GESV's generic specifications and civilian-grade components allowed manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce the vehicle with minimal tooling investment. This distributed production base contributed to the platform's resilience during the Jihad era, when targeted industrial strikes disrupted dedicated combat vehicle lines while GESV production continued at secondary and civilian facilities with little interruption.`,`Generic Expandable Services Vehicle`,{front:15,left:10,right:10,rear:15,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`communications-equipment`,`remote-sensor-dispenser`,{structuralTechRating:`e`,engine:`fuel-cell`,engineTechRating:`e`,fuelTons:3.5,chassisModifications:[`tractor`,`omni`],bar:7,armorTechRating:`e`,fireControl:`none`},{front:27,left:20,right:20,rear:25,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#2`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#3`,!0,`machine-gun`],{manufacturer:`Johnston Industries`,primaryFactory:`New Syrtis`},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#0`],[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Nashan Diversified`,primaryFactory:`Furillo`,communications:`Nashan Optichat`,targeting:`Nashan T-800 Series 4`},{engine:`Nashan Generation 5.26`,armor:`Nashan ArcShield Heavy Indi`},{front:11,left:7,right:7,rear:4,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#1`,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#2`,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#3`,null],`ammo-is-light-gauss-rifle-standard`,{structuralTechRating:`e`,engine:`ice`,engineTechRating:`e`,fuelTons:0,chassisModifications:[`armored`,`amphibious`],bar:10,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`advanced`},`The Sturmvogel Maritime Patrol WiGE is Nashan Diversified's answer to the coastal patrol and maritime security needs of the Lyran Alliance following its secession from the Federated Commonwealth. Originally conceived as a military gunship, the design was refocused toward coast guard and militia applications when Nashan recognized broader commercial potential in a fast, versatile patrol craft. Built on Furillo and introduced in 3063, the Sturmvogel, whose name translates roughly as Storm Bird, quickly earned a reputation for flexibility and endurance, serving in conditions ranging from tropical island chains to subarctic coastlines. Its well-publicized performance during the Word of Blake siege of Tharkad cemented its standing as a valuable light patrol asset, expanding its market to coast guards and planetary militias well beyond Lyran territory.`,`The Sturmvogel's primary asset is speed. Its fusion power plant drives the craft to a maximum speed exceeding 120 kilometers per hour with an unlimited operational range, making it well suited to extended patrol missions far from support facilities. The main armament is a single rotary autocannon paired with a battery of four mine dispensers, a combination that lets crews engage pursuing vessels directly or lay area-denial fields to channel enemies into kill zones. An onboard remote sensor dispenser supports search and rescue operations, helping detect missing vessels and hidden contacts across wide stretches of coastline and open water.

A cargo bay can carry rescue equipment on patrol missions or be converted for a boarding party when interdiction operations require it. The craft's modular architecture makes swapping weapons and equipment straightforward, allowing rapid reconfiguration for different mission profiles.

The Sturmvogel's most significant weakness is its light armor. Crews compensate by exploiting the WiGE's mobility over terrain that would stop conventional vehicles, avoiding direct engagement whenever possible. The design was never intended for front-line combat, and commanders who use it in that role pay a steep price.`,`Nashan Diversified began development of the Sturmvogel in the late 3050s, initially pursuing a military gunship contract. The original prototype carried a light Gauss rifle and mine dispensers, but Lyran evaluators found the armor protection insufficient for front-line service. Rather than abandon the project, Nashan redesigned the craft for coast guard and militia service, a market growing steadily since access to the Federated Suns-built Fulmar and Cormorant became uncertain following the Lyran Alliance's secession in 3058.

The Maritime Patrol variant entered production on Furillo by 3063 and spread quickly through Lyran coast guard units and planetary militias. Its reputation changed significantly during the Word of Blake siege of Tharkad, where Sturmvogels served in virtually every conceivable role, from direct combat to ferrying troops, search and rescue, medevac, and acting as detection platforms for ambushes against Blakist forces across the Tatyana Islands, the New Bismarck Archipelago, the industrial terrain of Heidelberg, and the frozen wastes of Franz and Sutherland. The tri-vid footage and testimonials from Tharkad drove sales sharply upward, pushing the craft into service with coast guards and militias throughout the Alliance and beyond.

The Sturmvogel's commercial success persuaded Nashan to develop faster, more capable successors. Partnering with Johnson Industries, the company produced the Fensalir combat WiGE in 3084, followed by the Swallow attack WiGE in 3092. Both designs trace their lineage to the Sturmvogel's hull architecture, confirming the Maritime Patrol's enduring influence on Lyran WiGE development.`,`Sturmvogel Maritime Patrol WiGE`,[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#4`,null],`ammo-is-rotary-ac-2-standard`,`ammo-is-lb-10x-standard`,[`quirk-poor-performance`,null]],designs:JSON.parse(`{"0":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Bordello Military Goods, Inc.|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Hadrian Mechanized Industries|Quikscell Company|Aldis Industries","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Antwerp|Ares|Kalidasa|Layover|Oliver|Pandora|Pompey|Richvale|Terra"},"components":"~0","text":{"overview":"The AC/2 Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle designed to provide long-range ballistic fire support at minimal cost. Part of the broader family of weapons carrier vehicles that originated during the Age of War, the AC/2 Carrier dates back to approximately 2520 in its primitive form, with the modernized version entering service alongside its missile carrier cousins. Built by Quikscell and numerous other manufacturers across the Inner Sphere, the vehicle fills a niche as cheap, expendable suppressive fire support, using volume of autocannon fire to compensate for the relatively modest damage output of individual Class 2 weapons.","capabilities":"The primitive AC/2 Carrier represents the earliest member of the weapons carrier family to mount ballistic weapons, entering service around 2520. Built as a fifty-five-ton support vehicle using primitive construction methods, it mounts four Class 2 autocannons behind BAR 6 armor with advanced fire control systems that partially compensate for the crude platform.\\n\\nThe design's straightforward maintenance requirements made it accessible to militaries with limited technical infrastructure. These vehicles were rendered obsolete by modernized carriers around 2580 but served through the Age of War era, establishing the ballistic carrier concept alongside the earlier missile carrier designs.","deployment":"The primitive AC/2 Carrier served during the Age of War, providing long-range ballistic fire support for armies still relying on pre-spaceflight military technology. Quik Products Inc manufactured these vehicles alongside the primitive missile carriers, establishing the full weapons carrier family that would endure for centuries.","battleHistory":"The AC/2 Carrier traces its lineage to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed a family of standardized tracked weapons platforms. The primitive ancestor entered service around 2520 mounting four Class 2 autocannons behind BAR 6 armor with advanced fire control systems. Despite crude construction, the design proved effective enough to inspire the modernized version that served through the Star League era and beyond. Like the missile carriers, the AC/2 Carrier suffered from widespread unauthorized copying during the Succession Wars, and Quikscell's post-Fourth Succession War legal campaign shut down many competitors.\\n\\nProduction spread across an extensive network. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Richvale formed the core, supplemented by Hadrian Mechanized Industries on Pompey, Aldis Industries on Terra, Bordello Military Goods on Antwerp, and Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain. This distributed base mirrored the missile carrier network.\\n\\nThe AC/2 Carrier received its first major modernization in 3063 with the LB-X variant. This substantial redesign replaced the internal combustion engine with a fusion powerplant, doubled armor protection, and substituted the standard autocannons with LB-X Class 2 weapons capable of firing both solid slugs and cluster ammunition. A C3 slave computer enabled networked targeting. Quikscell produced the LB-X variant at most of its carrier plants.\\n\\nDespite its long service history, the AC/2 Carrier has always occupied a somewhat ambiguous position within the weapons carrier family. The vehicle's similarity to the Pike support vehicle has caused persistent identification problems in the field. However, the AC/2 Carrier continues to fill its role as cheap long-range suppressive fire support, its simplicity and low cost ensuring continued relevance."}},"quirks":"~1","name":"AC/2 Carrier","model":"(Primitive)","tonnage":55,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":"~2","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["autocannon-standard-a","front","#0",null],["autocannon-standard-a","front","#1",null],["autocannon-standard-a","front","#2",null],["autocannon-standard-a","front","#3",null],["ammo-ac-2-standard",null,"#4","autocannon-standard-a"],["ammo-ac-2-standard",null,"#5","autocannon-standard-a"]]},"supportVehicle":"~4"},"1":{"tro":{"production":"~5","components":{"engine":"ICE (rating unknown)","armor":"BAR 6 (5 tons)"},"text":{"overview":"The Apostle is an eighty-ton primitive self-propelled artillery vehicle commissioned by the Terran Hegemony in 2321. Designed alongside its lighter companion, the Reaper, the Apostle represented the heavier of two artillery platforms developed to modernize the Hegemony Armed Forces' outdated indirect fire capabilities. Intended to provide long-range heavy fire support at the division level, the vehicle was assigned to battalion-sized artillery formations supporting HAF divisions in the field. As one of the earliest purpose-built self-propelled guns fielded by the Hegemony, the Apostle established a new standard for mobile artillery support that would influence Hegemony military doctrine for generations to come.","capabilities":"The Apostle's primary armament is a single Sniper artillery piece mounted in the hull, supported by three tons of ammunition providing thirty rounds of sustained fire. An advanced fire control system guides the weapon, enabling accurate engagement of targets at extreme range, while the ammunition capacity permits the crew to carry multiple munition types as the tactical situation demands. A single front-mounted small laser provides minimal self-defense capability against threats that manage to reach the gun line.\\n\\nPowered by an internal combustion engine, the Apostle achieves a cruise speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour with a flank speed of fifty-four. Its generous fuel supply provides an operational range of nearly eleven hundred kilometers, well suited to extended campaigns. Five tons of BAR 6 armor afford basic protection, reflecting the expectation that the vehicle would operate well behind the front lines rather than engage in direct combat. A tractor chassis modification allows the Apostle to tow additional equipment or ammunition trailers, extending its logistical footprint. The large crew of ten, including two officers and six dedicated gunners, ensures smooth operation of the complex artillery system.","deployment":"The Apostle Self-Propelled Artillery served as the primary heavy indirect fire platform for Hegemony Armed Forces division-level operations. Its substantial ammunition capacity and long operational range made it well suited to sustained bombardment campaigns in support of frontline formations. The tractor chassis allowed Apostle batteries to tow additional supplies during extended operations, making the vehicle the centerpiece of HAF heavy artillery battalions.","battleHistory":"The Apostle and Reaper emerged from a period of urgent military reform following James McKenna's rise to power as the first Director-General of the Terran Hegemony. Field Marshal Mears Coblitz, retained as Commanding General, recognized that the fractured remnants of the old Terran Alliance military desperately needed modernization to prevent the Hegemony from descending into civil war. Among his priorities, the artillery corps had long suffered from neglect, relying on outdated static, towed, and self-propelled pieces inherited from the Alliance era.\\n\\nCoblitz personally drafted requirements for two new self-propelled artillery vehicles. The Apostle filled the heavier role, intended for assignment to division-level artillery battalions, while the Reaper served a lighter requirement at the regimental and brigade level. Both vehicles entered service remarkably quickly, debuting in less than two years from initial requirements. The Apostle first saw combat during the later stages of the Campaign of Persuasion, as the Hegemony worked to expand and strengthen its influence across nearby star systems.\\n\\nThroughout the late twenty-third century and the decades that followed, the Apostle and Reaper gradually supplanted virtually all of the older Alliance artillery still in service. Both platforms remained in frontline use for more than a century before advancing technology rendered them obsolete. The Apostle received its formal obsolescence designation in 2485, replaced by newer designs incorporating standard rather than primitive technology. Despite this, a handful of examples survived into the thirty-first century. By the dawn of the Dark Age, at least a few Apostles remained as ceremonial vehicles stationed on Terra and New Earth."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-obsolete",2485],["quirk-improved-communications",null]]},"name":"Apostle Self-Propelled Artillery","tonnage":80,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":14,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~6",["sniper-artillery","front","#1",null],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#2","sniper-artillery"],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#3","sniper-artillery"],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#4","sniper-artillery"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":3,"chassisModifications":["tractor"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"advanced"}},"2":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Quik Products Inc.|Quik Products Inc.|Aldis Industries","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Ares|Pandora|Terra"},"components":{"engine":"Primitive ICE","armor":"BAR"},"text":{"overview":"The Armored Personnel Carrier is a ubiquitous ten-ton combat transport found in virtually every military force across the Inner Sphere and Periphery. Produced in three standard configurations -- hover, tracked, and wheeled -- these vehicles serve as the primary means of delivering infantry squads to and from the combat zone. The basic designs date back to the Star League era around 2500, with primitive predecessors operating as early as 2390, and production has continued without interruption for centuries. Their simplicity, low cost, and ease of manufacture ensure that every House military, mercenary command, and planetary militia fields them in quantity, making them among the most commonly encountered military vehicles in the galaxy.","capabilities":"The primitive tracked APC dates to approximately 2390, representing one of the earliest purpose-built military personnel carriers. Using civilian drive train components and a petrochemical-powered internal combustion engine, this ten-ton vehicle carries one ton of infantry -- a single squad -- at a maximum speed of only approximately fifty-four kilometers per hour. Two turret-mounted machine guns provide basic self-defense.\\n\\nConstructed with barrier armor rating six protection -- twenty-four points of armor coverage -- the primitive tracked APC offers better protection than the hover primitive but at a significant cost in mobility. The extremely low speed leaves the vehicle vulnerable during approach and withdrawal phases, and the reliance on petrochemical fuel limits operational endurance. Despite these limitations, the tracked primitive remained in service with the most resource-constrained forces for centuries, its simplicity and ease of manufacture ensuring continued production long after superior designs became available.","deployment":"The primitive tracked APC persists in service with militia and paramilitary forces on worlds where more advanced vehicles are unavailable or too expensive to maintain. The Draconis Combine and Free Worlds League both maintain tracked cargo variants derived from the primitive chassis for rear-area logistics. The design's extremely low speed limits its tactical utility, but its simplicity and reliability ensure continued production on the most resource-constrained worlds.","battleHistory":"The armored personnel carrier concept predates the modern Star League designs by over a century. Primitive APCs first appeared around 2390, using civilian drive train components and petrochemical-powered internal combustion engines to deliver troops under fire. Though crude by later standards, these early carriers established the fundamental template -- a lightly armed, armored box on wheels, tracks, or a hoverskirt -- that would endure for centuries. The Age of War and the technological advances it brought rendered these primitive designs obsolete for front-line service, but they never fully disappeared, remaining in use with militia and paramilitary forces well into the thirty-first century.\\n\\nThe modern APC entered service around 2500, when Star League-era manufacturers standardized three motive configurations optimized for different operational environments. Production spread rapidly across the Inner Sphere, with virtually every military manufacturer offering some version of the basic design. During the Succession Wars, demand far outstripped supply, and APCs became one of the few military vehicles that even the most devastated economies could continue producing. Hundreds of local manufacturers emerged to fill the gap, many working from incomplete specifications or reverse-engineered examples.\\n\\nThe standard APC's enduring legacy owes as much to its limitations as its capabilities. Its minimal carrying capacity of a single infantry squad pushed militaries to develop heavier alternatives, while its thin armor and light armament drove the creation of numerous weapon-platform conversions. Militia commanders, unable to afford purpose-built combat vehicles, frequently stripped the infantry compartment to mount LRM or SRM launchers, creating improvised fire support vehicles. The Taurian Concordat and Marian Hegemony independently developed sensor-equipped hover variants for border patrol duties. One notable action occurred in 3034, when Overste-Lojtnant Illya Stroncek of the Lyran Commonwealth led a column of tracked APCs through Kurita lines on Last Frontier, evacuating three companies of wounded infantry under sustained fire and earning the Order of the Griffin for her actions."}},"name":"Armored Personnel Carrier","model":"(Tracked Primitive)","tonnage":10,"troopSpace":1,"armorAllocation":{"front":5,"left":5,"right":5,"rear":4,"turret":5,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~7","$v":[["machine-gun","turret","#0",null,null],["machine-gun","turret","#1",null,null],["~8",null,"#2",true,"machine-gun"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"3":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Sheinhardt Universal Technologies","primaryFactory":"Sirius V"},"components":{"engine":"ICE (Unknown)"},"text":{"overview":"The Asher Hover Scout emerged in 2298 as the debut military product from Sheinhardt Universal Technologies, a company better known for agricultural and industrial logistics than battlefield hardware. The design originated as a commercial failure, a heavy freight hauler that found no market in its intended role, repurposed by SUT engineers who installed a more capable engine, added weaponry, and bolted on several tons of armor. Marketed to planetary militias across the Terran Alliance as a cost-effective scouting solution, the vehicle found early acceptance with the Alliance Global Militia, whose initial procurement validated SUT's aggressive entry into defense manufacturing. The small number of Ashers built directly by SUT on Sirius were regarded as capable and reliable machines, earning genuine praise from experienced operators, though the company's reliance on a broad subcontractor network to meet demand created quality inconsistencies that shadowed the design's reputation throughout its service life. The Asher remained a common, if imperfect, fixture of early twenty-fourth century militia forces.","capabilities":"The Asher is a 45-ton medium hover vehicle capable of a top speed of 129 kph, providing adequate mobility for reconnaissance and screening operations in open terrain, though operators found its handling unreliable at high speed due to a persistent tendency to yaw uncontrollably. Its main offensive punch comes from a ten-tube long-range missile launcher mounted in the turret, giving the vehicle the ability to engage targets at significant range before closing. Machine guns and flamethrowers supplement the missile armament, making the Asher effective against infantry and light vehicle targets at close quarters. The turret provides reasonable firing arcs, though a mechanical tendency for the traverse mechanism to slow or freeze during extended operations reduced reliability in sustained engagements.\\n\\nThe vehicle's armor protection is comparatively heavy for its class, generally sufficient to absorb punishment that would destroy lighter scouts outright. This durability made it popular in a heavy cavalry role beyond its nominal reconnaissance mission, as field commanders valued its ability to press engagements where more fragile platforms could not. The primary tactical limitation was logistical: the widespread use of subcontractors across SUT's production network meant that replacement components from one manufacturer frequently proved incompatible with vehicles from another, complicating repair operations for multi-source regiments. Sirius-built examples avoided most of these issues, as SUT's own production line maintained consistent tolerances.","deployment":"The Asher Hover Scout served with planetary militia formations throughout the Terran Alliance and, through SUT's subsidiary distribution network, with forces aligned with each of the five Great Houses during the twenty-fourth century. Within the Alliance Global Militia, the vehicle's above-average armor for its class pushed it toward a heavy cavalry role rather than pure reconnaissance, as unit commanders valued its ability to survive contact that would destroy lighter scouts. Regiments operating Sirius-built examples generally assigned the Asher to its intended scouting mission, while those receiving subcontractor-built vehicles more often used them in screening and support duties where precision handling mattered less. AGM crews developed the informal nickname \\"Ten-Pack\\" for the vehicle, referencing both its ten-tube missile launcher and its notorious tendency to handle unpredictably under hard maneuvering. Surviving examples continued in local service after SUT's dissolution, gradually declining in operational readiness as the parts incompatibility problems left by the multi-source production network took their toll on fleet maintenance.","battleHistory":"Sheinhardt Universal Technologies designed the original vehicle as a heavy hover transport for agricultural and industrial cargo, but the design attracted no meaningful commercial interest. Rather than abandon the development investment, SUT engineers converted the platform for military use, fitting a more powerful engine, adding a ten-tube long-range missile launcher along with machine guns and flamethrowers, and bolting on additional armor. The resulting vehicle, designated the AS-H and marketed as the Armed Scout-Hover, entered production on Sirius in 2298.\\n\\nSUT built four demonstration models to secure initial procurement interest. Alliance Global Militia evaluators responded favorably, their enthusiasm reportedly assisted by substantial corporate donations made on the company's behalf to militia officials. The AGM placed an initial order of five thousand units, a figure that far exceeded SUT's own production capacity on Sirius. The company responded by subcontracting production across dozens of subsidiary firms distributed throughout the Terran Alliance and beyond, few of which applied consistent quality control measures or used standardized components. Ashers produced directly by SUT on Sirius were consistently regarded as well-built machines, earning high marks from their crews. The far larger subcontractor-built portion of the fleet represented a different story, assembled from incompatible components that made maintenance difficult and reliability unpredictable.\\n\\nAGM regiments receiving mixed-source fleets found that spare parts rarely transferred between vehicles of nominally identical specification, complicating logistics across the force. Despite these problems, the Asher remained in production for several decades. SUT's subsidiaries marketed cosmetic variations of the design to all five Great Houses throughout the twenty-fourth century, cultivating sales relationships across interstellar political boundaries. This practice eventually attracted the attention of the Terran Hegemony government, which governed commercial activity on Sirius and viewed SUT's multi-faction marketing of military hardware as a violation of Terran commercial law. The Hegemony declared SUT in open violation of its statutes, dissolved the company, and auctioned its assets across the realm. Military historians have since theorized that a substantial portion of those assets were eventually acquired by subsidiaries of the Quikscell Company, whose low-cost vehicle manufacturing practices bore a recognizable resemblance to SUT's operating model.\\n\\nProduction of the Asher ended permanently with SUT's dissolution. No successor firm resumed the line, and vehicles already in service continued operating wherever they had been distributed, sustained by locally available components for as long as maintenance remained possible. The chronic parts incompatibility left by the subcontractor network made long-term sustainment increasingly difficult for regiments operating large mixed-source fleets."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~9",["quirk-obsolete",2360],"~10"]},"name":"Asher Hover Scout","tonnage":45,"motiveType":"hover","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":11,"left":11,"right":11,"rear":6,"turret":10,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~11",["vehicle-flamer","front","#1",null],"~12",["lrm-5","turret","#3",null],["machine-gun","turret","#4",null],["machine-gun","turret","#5",null],["~8",null,"#6","machine-gun"],["ammo-vehicle-flamer-standard",null,"#7","vehicle-flamer"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#8","lrm-5"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":2,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"4":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Dortmein Wagons Ltd.","primaryFactory":"Arcturus"},"components":{"engine":"Unknown (Primitive ICE)","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Carter Medical Emergency Response Vehicle, introduced by Dortmein Wagons, Ltd. on Arcturus in 2341, represents an early Lyran Commonwealth effort to provide organized, mobile battlefield medical care at the regimental level. Developed as a primitive prototype rather than a production design, the Carter MERV was built to deliver surgical-grade treatment far forward from fixed medical installations. Found in both military formations and civilian emergency services organizations across the Inner Sphere, the vehicle earned a reputation as a practical and accessible platform for mobile medical teams despite its primitive construction. Though it eventually gave way to more advanced successors, the Carter MERV served as an early model for the wheeled battlefield medicine concept that would persist across multiple eras.","capabilities":"The Carter MERV is a 25-ton wheeled vehicle powered by a primitive internal combustion engine, capable of cruising at modest speeds with off-road mobility provided by its modified chassis and control systems. Unlike conventional combat vehicles, the MERV carries no weapons, dedicating all available mass to medical equipment, crew accommodations, and operational supplies. Its most distinctive feature is an expanding pop-out structural design that allows a compact road footprint to unfold into a substantially larger working space when deployed in the field. This configuration permits a crew of fifteen, including three officers, to operate simultaneously across four interior compartments.\\n\\nThe forward command section serves as both the driver's compartment and the management station for the senior medical officer. The rear compartment handles triage and walking-wounded cases, with capacity for approximately two tons of cargo or up to twenty unequipped personnel. The center section functions as an emergency room with four treatment bays, each served by paramedic-grade diagnostic and monitoring equipment. Two operating theaters, one fore and one aft of the center section, provide dedicated surgical capability, with one full set of MASH equipment servicing both theaters.\\n\\nThe vehicle's armor provides protection appropriate to a non-combatant platform designed to operate behind the forward line of engagement. Nominal facing armor is adequate to deflect stray small arms fire and shell fragments but offers little resistance against dedicated anti-vehicle weaponry. The ICE powerplant, while fuel-intensive and demanding of resupply, is straightforward to service and diagnose in the field, and the vehicle's overall construction earned a formal designation as easy to maintain even by the primitive engineering standards of its era.","deployment":"The Carter MERV was designed to deploy in groups of two to twelve vehicles, with larger formations providing comprehensive medical coverage for brigade-scale operations. In military service, individual MERVs attached to regimental medical trains were typically positioned well to the rear of the forward engagement line, operating under whatever local arrangement protected non-combatant vehicles while remaining close enough to receive casualties quickly.\\n\\nCivilian emergency services organizations across the Inner Sphere adopted the design wherever mobile forward-deployed medical care was required, particularly on worlds lacking the infrastructure for permanent trauma centers. The vehicle's wheeled mobility and off-road capability allowed it to reach incident sites across a wide range of terrain. The Carter MERV's low technical maintenance burden made it especially attractive to planetary defense forces and independent emergency services that lacked access to advanced technical support personnel, keeping examples in service long after the design's nominal obsolescence date.","battleHistory":"The Carter MERV emerged from centuries of hard-won battlefield medical doctrine. Military commanders had long understood that survival rates for combat casualties improved dramatically when trained medical personnel reached the wounded quickly. This recognition drove continuous evolution in field medicine, from static regimental aid stations to air-transportable MASH units, then VTOL air ambulances, fixed-wing air hospitals, and dedicated hospital DropShips. By the early thirty-fourth century of the Common Era, military theorists had begun cycling back toward highly mobile ground vehicles as an ideal complement to those larger assets, capable of moving under their own power directly to forward casualty collection points without the logistical complexity of air operations.\\n\\nDortmein Wagons, Ltd., a manufacturer based in the Lyran Commonwealth on Arcturus, developed the Carter MERV in 2341 under the direction of supervising engineer Minka Albrecht. The project was classified as non-production equipment from its inception, intended as a proving ground for the self-contained wheeled medical facility concept rather than a candidate for series manufacture. The timing placed its development in the formative years of the Lyran Commonwealth, as that state was consolidating its holdings and building the logistical infrastructure necessary to support a major regional military. Battlefield medicine, long a secondary concern in frontier conflicts, had become a recognized priority as the scale of potential operations between the emerging Successor States grew.\\n\\nDespite its non-production status, the Carter MERV's simple construction and accessible mechanical systems allowed it to remain in service well beyond the lifespan of more sophisticated contemporaries. Its design quirk records formally noted the vehicle as obsolete within the first century of its introduction as more capable platforms became available, but practical considerations kept examples operating in garrison and support roles for decades past that designation. As the Star League era progressed and the Succession Wars followed, primitive systems and limited operational range gradually excluded the Carter MERV from serious front-line consideration. By the later Succession Wars, the design was found primarily in the inventories of low-technology planetary defense forces and civilian emergency organizations where technical simplicity outweighed performance limitations. The design's lasting contribution lay not in its production numbers but in demonstrating that a wheeled, crew-intensive medical platform could operate effectively far forward of permanent installations, a lesson that informed generations of successor designs."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~13",["quirk-obsolete",2580],["quirk-gas-hog",null]]},"name":"Carter Medical Emergency Response Vehicle","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":2,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":14,"right":14,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~14","$v":[["mash-core","#0",null],["paramedic-equipment","#1",null],["paramedic-equipment","#2","front"],["paramedic-equipment","#3","right"],["paramedic-equipment","#4","left"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":["off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"5":{"tro":{"production":"~15","components":{"engine":"ICE","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"~16","capabilities":"~17","deployment":"The original production model of the Cobra VTOL Transport, entering service with the Hegemony Armed Forces in 2317. Built around the standard tilt-rotor airframe with an internal combustion power plant, the baseline configuration carries nine tons of cargo across three configurable hold sections and supports a slung load for heavier deliveries. Field conversion kits were developed in parallel with the original design, allowing individual vehicles to serve in a range of support roles without dedicated workshop support. The standard Cobra was produced in quantity and issued across all branches of Hegemony forces, with large numbers eventually demilitarized and sold to civilian operators following decommissioning.","battleHistory":"~18"}},"quirks":"~19","name":"Cobra VTOL Transport","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":9,"cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~20","equipment":"~21","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"6":{"tro":{"production":"~15","components":{"engine":"Fusion","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"~16","capabilities":"~17","deployment":"The final military production variant of the original Cobra line, fulfilling the last Hegemony Armed Forces orders from 2489. Replacing the baseline internal combustion engine with a fusion power plant, the Fusion configuration achieves roughly a twenty-five percent improvement in top speed and a modest increase in cargo capacity relative to the original model. This variant represents the design's operational peak within the original airframe and marks the endpoint of Pacific-Euro's Cobra production before the line was permanently discontinued.","battleHistory":"~18"}},"quirks":"~19","name":"Cobra VTOL Transport","model":"(Fusion)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":9.5,"cruiseMP":10,"armorAllocation":"~20","equipment":"~21","supportVehicle":"~22"},"7":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell"},"text":{"overview":"~23","capabilities":"~24","deployment":"The Command Post configuration equips the GESV tractor with seven tons of communication equipment and two Remote Sensor Dispensers, transforming the vehicle into a mobile headquarters. Requiring a nine-person crew, this configuration is deployed with command-level units at the company, battalion, and regimental echelons to provide flexible communications infrastructure in the field. Its mobility allows command elements to reposition frequently, reducing vulnerability to counter-battery fire and enemy intelligence targeting.","battleHistory":"~25"}},"name":"~26","model":"(Command Post)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":3,"doors":1}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~27","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","pod","location"],"$v":[["~28","#0",true,null],["~29","#1",true,"rear"],["~29","#2",true,"rear"]]},"supportVehicle":"~30"},"8":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell"},"text":{"overview":"~23","capabilities":"~24","deployment":"The Field Kitchen configuration mounts two field kitchens alongside refrigerated and standard cargo to support sustained field operations. With an expanded kitchen crew of eight, the configuration feeds upward of 300 personnel per service. It is deployed primarily with large rear-echelon formations, refugee operations, and disaster relief missions where providing hot meals across a broad population is a significant morale and operational factor.","battleHistory":"~25"}},"name":"~26","model":"(Field Kitchen)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":{"$k":["tag","amount","doors"],"$v":[["cargo",3,1],["cargo-refrigerated",1.7,1]]},"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~27","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","pod"],"$v":[["field-kitchen","#0",true],["field-kitchen","#1",true]]},"supportVehicle":"~30"},"9":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell"},"text":{"overview":"~23","capabilities":"~24","deployment":"The MASH configuration equips the GESV with a full MASH unit and six surgical theaters, supported by a medical complement of 36 personnel. It provides organic surgical capability to formations operating beyond the range of fixed medical facilities. The configuration is deployed immediately behind front-line units to minimize patient transit time, and is frequently paired with GESV Trailer bunk configurations to expand patient housing capacity.","battleHistory":"~25"}},"name":"~26","model":"(MASH)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":2.5,"doors":1}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~27","equipment":[{"tag":"mash-core","uuid":"#0","pod":true}],"supportVehicle":"~30"},"10":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"~23","capabilities":"The GESV Trailer is a fifteen-ton OmniVehicle designed as a dedicated companion to the GESV tractor. Built on an Omni chassis, the trailer is protected by one and a half tons of BAR 7 armor, sufficient to shield cargo and occupants against incidental fire and debris during rear-echelon operations. The trailer carries no engine of its own, relying entirely on the tractor for movement.\\n\\nThe trailer's OmniVehicle architecture provides full reconfiguration flexibility within available pod space. Unlike traditional cargo or personnel trailers, which commit to a single function from manufacture, the GESV Trailer's interior can be arranged into virtually any configuration that fits within tonnage limits. This makes the platform adaptable to shifting operational priorities without requiring different trailer types in the logistics chain.\\n\\nTwo GESV Trailers can be coupled and pulled simultaneously by a single tractor, allowing commanders to combine functions, such as pairing bunk space with a workspace module, to support diverse unit requirements from a single prime mover. The principal limitation of the trailer is its complete dependence on the tractor for mobility. Without the tractor, the trailer is stationary, which makes initial positioning decisions critical in dynamic operational environments.","deployment":"The Cargo configuration maximizes available interior space for general logistics transport, with a capacity of up to eleven and a half tons. The flexible interior can also serve as a workspace for intelligence personnel, staff officers, or technical specialists requiring office space in the field. It is the most versatile of the trailer configurations and is frequently deployed with any of the tractor configurations depending on the current operational requirement.","battleHistory":"~25"}},"name":"Generic Expandable Services Vehicle Trailer","model":"(Cargo)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":11.5,"doors":1}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":0,"armorAllocation":{"front":8,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","trailer","omni"],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"11":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"ScolTek Associates","primaryFactory":"Inarcs"},"text":{"overview":"The Hi-Scout drones are remote-controlled reconnaissance vehicles produced by ScolTek Associates on Inarcs as integral components of the Hi-Scout Drone Carrier system. Introduced in 3000 for the Lyran Commonwealth Armed Forces, the drones were designed to extend the carrier's sensor network across a wide area without risking manned vehicles in hostile territory. Produced in two complementary variants, the hover-based NapFind and tracked PathTrak, these small unmanned platforms work in concert with their parent vehicle to create overlapping sensor chains capable of covering far more ground than the carrier alone. Though lightly built and essentially defenseless, the drones proved remarkably effective during the late Third Succession War, when sophisticated detection equipment was scarce across the Inner Sphere. ScolTek's design philosophy emphasized sensor capability and speed over durability, producing platforms that were expendable by intent but operationally invaluable to the commanders who employed them.","capabilities":"Both drone types share a common remote control architecture linked to the Hi-Scout carrier's StealthMat-Q communications system with MultiTrack coordination. This allows the carrier crew to manage all six drones simultaneously across separate sensor channels. The drones relay their findings in real time, building a composite picture of the operational area that far exceeds what any single vehicle could achieve. The system's primary limitation is its vulnerability to high levels of background radiation, which can disrupt the MultiTrack coordination link and leave drones unable to communicate with their parent vehicle.\\n\\nThe NapFind is a two-ton hover platform built for speed, capable of rapidly covering open terrain to establish initial contact with enemy forces. Its sensor package includes infrared, seismic, sound, and motion detection. The PathTrak is a heavier three-ton tracked platform with a more comprehensive sensor loadout, including the ability to intercept enemy radio transmissions, making it the primary intelligence-gathering element in the drone chain. Both variants carry minimal armor protection, sufficient only to shield their electronics from environmental hazards rather than hostile fire. Neither drone mounts weapons capable of engaging enemy combat units, and both rely entirely on speed, small size, and the disposable nature of unmanned platforms to survive on the battlefield.","deployment":"The NapFind is a two-ton hover drone designed for rapid area coverage. Employing a fuel cell engine and an advanced venturi-disk skirtless hover system, it achieves speeds approaching 250 kilometers per hour, allowing it to quickly sweep large areas and report contacts back to the carrier. The NapFind operates at ranges of thirty to fifty kilometers from its parent vehicle, using intermediary drones as relay stations when working at extended distances. Its sensor range of approximately ten kilometers is shorter than the PathTrak's, but the NapFind's speed allows it to cover proportionally more ground in the same time. Armed only with a pair of manipulators used primarily for demolitions work or hazmat handling, the NapFind has no combat capability and relies entirely on speed and its small profile to avoid engagement.","battleHistory":"ScolTek Associates developed the Hi-Scout drone family as part of an integrated reconnaissance system designed to fill a critical intelligence gap in LCAF planetary defense. During the late Third Succession War, dedicated scout vehicles were rare and BattleMechs assigned to reconnaissance roles were increasingly vulnerable to combined-arms ambushes. The drones offered a disposable alternative that preserved both expensive equipment and irreplaceable MechWarriors.\\n\\nThe NapFind and PathTrak entered service together in 3000, purpose-built to work as complementary halves of a sensor network. Early deployments proved the concept sound, and the LCAF quickly expanded Hi-Scout formations across garrison commands on less strategically vital worlds. The system's value as a ground-based early warning network made it particularly attractive for planets that lacked satellite reconnaissance coverage, a common situation during the technology-poor late Succession Wars era.\\n\\nThe drones' relatively simple construction made them vulnerable to reverse engineering. After the first LCAF Hi-Scouts were captured during a Kurita raid, functional copies and knock-off designs appeared across the Inner Sphere within two decades. ScolTek continued producing the original drones at full capacity through the Jihad, suffering no damage to their Inarcs facilities from the Word of Blake or other hostile forces. The original drone designs remained in production alongside upgraded carrier variants well into the Dark Age, a reflection of the fundamental soundness of the platform despite its aging technology base."}},"name":"Hi-Scout Drone","model":"(NapFind)","tonnage":2.5,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.015,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":15,"armorAllocation":{"front":1,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":0,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~14","$v":[["drone-operating-system","#0",null],["manipulator","#1","front"],["manipulator","#2","front"],["seating-standard","#3",null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"a","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"f","fuelTons":0.033,"chassisModifications":["ultra-light"],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"a","fireControl":"none"}},"12":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"New Hessen ArmorWorks|Wu Industries|Wu Industries","primaryFactory":"New Hessen|Capricorn III|Sian"},"text":{"overview":"The Korvin Tank emerged in the early 2300s as the Capellan Confederation's purpose-built response to large laser technology. Constructed under the supervision of engineer Wu Han at New Hessen ArmorWorks on New Hessen, the design exploited reverse-engineered Terran Hegemony weapons to give Confederation forces a decisive edge over contemporary armored vehicles. Named in honor of explorer and philosopher Alana Korvin DeVall, the Korvin became the standard battle tank of the Capellan Confederation for much of the 24th century. A 50-ton tracked medium vehicle powered by a fusion engine, it combined energy firepower and solid speed in a package uncommon among vehicles of the era. The Korvin served Capellan forces for over two centuries across multiple production sites.","capabilities":"The Korvin's primary identity is as an energy weapon platform. The Wu Heavy Laser in the turret delivers considerable damage at medium and longer ranges without ammunition dependency, while a Longbow LRM-10 rack provides indirect fire support to engage targets beyond the laser's effective reach. Five Browning Large Caliber machine guns are positioned to cover all arcs of the vehicle, deterring infantry from closing. The Magna 250 fusion powerplant drives the 50-ton hull to a flank speed of 86 km/h, giving the Korvin the mobility to serve as a flanking unit or pursue retreating forces alongside heavier assets.\\n\\nEight tons of ProtecTech 4 armor offers reasonable protection for a medium vehicle, though it falls short of the most heavily armored vehicles in its weight class. The fusion engine, while providing a weight advantage over internal combustion rivals, proved demanding to maintain. Environmental controls and other internal systems are persistent reliability concerns, a consequence of the design's rushed development. The tank's unusual silhouette, another artifact of that rapid design process, makes it easily recognizable on the battlefield but offers no benefit in concealment.","deployment":"The KRV-2 served in the front-line armored regiments of the Capellan Confederation during the 24th century, where its large laser firepower gave it a genuine edge against the vehicles of the era. As BattleMechs came to dominate the battlefield and armor technology advanced, the KRV-2 transitioned to garrison and militia roles, serving roughly another century and a half in secondary assignments. With the introduction of the KRV-3, most surviving KRV-2s were sold to Periphery states rather than scrapped. Spare parts for the fusion reactor are now extremely scarce, and operational examples are exceptionally rare.","battleHistory":"The Korvin's origins trace to the Tikonov Grand Union, which spent decades contesting border systems with the Terran Hegemony. Despite numerous defeats, Tikonov engineers salvaged Hegemony technology from the battlefield and eventually replicated the large laser. Engineer Wu Han oversaw the translation of that achievement into a functional combat vehicle, producing the KRV-2 at New Hessen ArmorWorks in 2367. This second-generation design, named for the philosopher Alana Korvin DeVall, featured a lighter and more powerful fusion engine than its Hessen-series predecessor, increasing top speed by roughly thirty percent. Production later expanded to Capricorn III and Sian with the formation of Wu Industries.\\n\\nThe Korvin served as the backbone of Capellan armored forces through much of the 24th century. Assigned to premier regiments, it gave Confederation armor a genuine advantage in an era when few vehicles mounted adequate protection against large laser fire. The design's prominence helped shape early Capellan armored doctrine, and the tank became a symbol of Confederation military capability. As armor technology improved across the Inner Sphere, however, a single heavy laser was no longer sufficient to dominate the battlefield, and the Korvin was progressively shifted to garrison and militia duties.\\n\\nWu Industries introduced the KRV-3 in 2430, attempting to revitalize the aging design with improved performance. The new model performed adequately but could not reverse the Korvin's decline in the face of proliferating BattleMechs. Production on Capricorn III and Sian continued until Wu Industries closed its doors in 2612. Most remaining KRV-2s were sold to Periphery states rather than scrapped, while the design's influence reached even further when the Umayyad Caliphate began producing its own version on Granada in the 29th century.","notes":"Armor exceeds point limit for 50.0-ton Tracked support vehicle: 105 points > 104."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~9","~13",["quirk-obsolete",2612]]},"name":"Korvin Tank","model":"KRV-2","tonnage":50,"cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":22,"right":22,"rear":17,"turret":21,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~11",["large-laser","turret","#1",null],"~12",["~8",null,"#3","machine-gun"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#4","lrm-5"]]},"supportVehicle":"~22"},"13":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|King Dumount Defense Facility|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Aldis Industries|Amaris Armored Vehicles","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Ares|Broken Wheel|Castile|Kalidasa|Layover|Maxwell|Oliver|Pandora|Terra|Timbuktu"},"components":"~0","text":{"overview":"The LRM Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle designed to deliver massed long-range missile fire at minimal cost. Developed by the Quikscell Company as a modernized successor to primitive Age of War designs dating back to the 2440s, the current production model entered service around 2470 and has remained in continuous use ever since. Manufactured by nearly every major military supplier across the Inner Sphere, the LRM Carrier fills the role of cheap, expendable indirect fire support in armies that cannot afford dedicated artillery assets. Its reputation as a disposable weapons platform belies its effectiveness when properly employed in massed formations, where concentrated volleys of missiles can devastate even heavily armored targets.","capabilities":"The primitive LRM Carrier represents the original Age of War design developed by Quik Products Inc around 2440. Built as a fifty-five-ton support vehicle using primitive construction methods, it mounts three LRM-20 launchers behind BAR 6 armor rated for battlefield protection but far below modern military standards. The InterComBust 180 engine and basic fire control systems perform adequately but lack the refinements of later production models.\\n\\nAdvanced fire control systems partially compensate for the crude construction, and the design's straightforward maintenance requirements made it accessible to militaries with limited technical infrastructure. These vehicles were rendered obsolete by the modernized carriers that entered production around 2580, though a handful survived in Periphery arsenals for decades afterward.","deployment":"The primitive LRM Carrier served during the Age of War era, providing massed missile fire for armies still relying on pre-spaceflight military technology. Quik Products Inc manufactured these vehicles primarily on Ares and Pandora. The design was rendered obsolete by modernized carriers around 2580 but established the weapons carrier concept that would endure for centuries.","battleHistory":"The origins of the modern missile carrier stretch back to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed the first standardized tracked platforms for massed missile delivery. These early designs, constructed using primitive technology, were crude but effective, establishing the basic formula of maximum firepower on a minimal chassis that would define the weapons carrier concept for centuries. As Quik Products evolved into the Quikscell Company, the original primitive carriers were refined into the modern designs that entered widespread service by the twenty-fifth century.\\n\\nDuring the Succession Wars, the desperate need for cheap fire support led dozens of manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce their own versions, many of them little more than unauthorized copies of Quikscell designs built to even lower quality standards. Shortly before the Fourth Succession War, Quikscell launched a series of lawsuits against these clone manufacturers, successfully shutting down many smaller competitors and forcing larger firms to cease production of derivative designs. The company further consolidated its market dominance by offering to exchange any non-Quikscell carrier for legitimate replacement vehicles and parts, effectively cornering the missile carrier market. One company not targeted by the legal attacks was Joint Equipment Systems, which had negotiated in good faith with Quik Products Inc centuries before.\\n\\nLRM Carrier production spread across an extensive network of facilities. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Broken Wheel turned out thousands of units across every era. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, Amaris Armored Vehicles on Timbuktu, the King Dumount Defense Facility on Castile, and Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell all contributed to a production network spanning the entire Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe first significant modernization came in 3055, when Quikscell introduced an upgraded variant for the Federated Commonwealth. By incorporating ferro-fibrous armor and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each launcher, engineers dramatically improved missile accuracy while adding a C3 slave unit for networked targeting. The reduced engine size was considered an acceptable trade for the enhanced combat capability. Oliver and Richvale became the primary production sites for this upgrade package.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake produced their own radical revision during the Jihad era, utilizing an extralight fusion engine to boost both speed and armor protection while retaining Artemis IV systems and adding a C3i computer. Aldis Industries on Terra manufactured this variant throughout the Blakist occupation. In the Dark Age, Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion, mounting the launchers in a partial-traverse turret with hardened armor inspired by the success of the Viking IIC. The LRM Carrier's continued evolution across more than six centuries of service stands as sign of the enduring viability of cheap, massed missile platforms in modern warfare."}},"quirks":"~1","name":"LRM Carrier","model":"(Primitive)","tonnage":55,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":"~2","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["lrm-15","front","#0",null],["lrm-15","front","#1",null],["lrm-15","front","#2",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#3","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#4","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#5","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#6","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#7","lrm-15"]]},"supportVehicle":"~4"},"14":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Blue Bull, Inc."},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Nifty Transportable Field Repair Unit is an ICE-powered knockoff of Johnston Industries' JI-50 Jifty, produced by Blue Bull, Inc. during the Jihad era. Appearing in the latter half of the 3060s, the Nifty blatantly copied the Jifty's basic design while substituting a cheaper internal combustion engine for the original's fusion powerplant. The vehicle was marketed as a budget alternative to the Jifty for military organizations that could not afford or obtain the Federated Suns original. Despite legal challenges from Johnston Industries over patent violations, the chaos of the Jihad prevented any meaningful resolution, and the Nifty entered limited production.","capabilities":"The Nifty follows the Jifty's general layout as a tracked field repair vehicle, retaining the lift hoists and cargo capacity that define the role. Its most significant departure from the original is the substitution of an internal combustion engine for the Jifty's fusion powerplant. This reduces the vehicle's logistical independence, tying it to fossil fuel supply lines that the fusion-equipped Jifty avoids entirely. The cost savings are considerable, however, making the Nifty accessible to smaller militaries and mercenary commands that cannot justify the expense of a fusion-powered support vehicle.\\n\\nThe ICE powerplant also limits the Nifty's performance compared to its progenitor, though exact specifications remain poorly documented. Industrial-grade armor provides basic crew protection consistent with the vehicle's rear-echelon role. Like the Jifty, the Nifty is not designed for direct combat and relies on escorting forces for protection in contested areas. Its value lies in providing field repair capability to organizations that would otherwise lack any mobile maintenance platform.","deployment":"The Nifty saw limited adoption during the Jihad, primarily among smaller military organizations and independent commands unable to secure Johnston Industries' Jifty through normal procurement channels. Johnston Industries' JI-100, a larger and more capable successor to the Jifty introduced in 3074, proved that the original design concept was sound enough to scale upward. Military commands gradually upgraded from the JI-50 to the JI-100, and the Nifty found no lasting place on AFFS support rosters.","battleHistory":"Blue Bull, Inc. entered the field repair vehicle market in the latter half of the 3060s by copying Johnston Industries' JI-50 Jifty design. The company made no attempt to disguise the origins of their product, producing what was essentially a direct clone with a cheaper ICE powerplant substituted for the original's fusion engine. Johnston Industries initially attempted to stop the infringement through legal action, but the onset of the Jihad in 3067 made pursuing patent litigation effectively impossible.\\n\\nJohnston Industries' leadership quickly recognized that the courts offered no practical remedy and shifted strategy. Rather than continue an unwinnable legal battle, they directed their research and development resources toward creating a superior successor vehicle. The result was the JI-100 Transportable Recovery Repair Unit, introduced in the first quarter of 3074 to enthusiastic reception. The JI-100 massed 50 tons on a heavy tracked chassis with significantly greater cargo capacity than either the Jifty or the Nifty, positioning Johnston Industries firmly ahead of its imitator.\\n\\nThe Nifty's market position eroded steadily as the post-Jihad military rebuilding favored established manufacturers with proven supply chains. Blue Bull's inability to match Johnston's engineering improvements or factory capacity meant the Nifty remained a budget option in a market increasingly willing to pay for quality. The vehicle's legacy is primarily as a footnote in the Jifty's history, a reminder that wartime chaos creates opportunities for imitation that peacetime litigation would otherwise prevent."}},"name":"Nifty Transportable Field Repair Unit","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":19.5,"doors":1}],"era":"jihad","armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":{"$k":"~7","$v":[["machine-gun-light","front","#0",null,null],["machine-gun-light","front","#1",null,null],"~32",["lift-hoist","rear","#3",null,null],["ammo-is-light-machine-gun-standard",null,"#4",true,"machine-gun-light"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"15":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"GSB, Incorporated","primaryFactory":"Tiber"},"components":{"engine":"240 Fusion","armor":"BAR 6 Standard"},"text":{"overview":"The Paramour Mobile Repair Vehicle was introduced in 2557 by Gilbert, Sachs, and Belafonte, a Tiber-based conglomerate in the Free Worlds League. Designed during the period when the League prepared to join the Star League, the Paramour represented a deliberate strategic choice to invest in logistics rather than direct combat capability. GSB, Inc. applied its expertise in luxury rough-terrain vehicles to create a mobile repair platform that would keep FWLM units operating at peak readiness in the field. The result was a vehicle that combined comfort, reliability, and genuine utility in a sixty-ton tracked chassis. The FWLM was so impressed with the design that it purchased every Paramour produced. Though the vehicle's polished appearance sometimes drew mockery from combat formations, the Paramour earned lasting respect through the competence and professionalism of its crews.","capabilities":"The Paramour is built on a sixty-ton tracked chassis powered by a fusion engine capable of reaching speeds up to 65 km/h. This mobility allows it to keep pace with armored formations while carrying the tools and parts needed for field repairs. The vehicle's primary asset is its spacious cargo area, which holds fifteen tons of spare parts and equipment for a wide variety of maintenance tasks. A pair of lift hoists enables the Paramour to handle loads up to sixty tons each, sufficient to lift and reposition damaged BattleMechs or heavy combat vehicles on the battlefield.\\n\\nThe crew compartment accommodates three personnel, with quarters comfortable enough to serve as a mobile field headquarters for quartermasters. A five-ton infantry bay provides space for additional technicians or a security detail. For self-defense, the vehicle mounts a single GM MiniGun machine gun in the front, providing a visible deterrent without adding excessive weight. BAR 6 armor offers adequate protection against small arms fire and shrapnel, while the armored chassis and controls modification provides additional resilience. The Paramour was never intended to see direct combat, and its light armament reflects that design philosophy.","deployment":"The base Paramour was deployed exclusively with Free Worlds League Military formations from its introduction in 2557 through the Star League era and into the Succession Wars. As a dedicated logistics platform, it served in support echelons behind the front lines, maintaining and repairing BattleMechs and vehicles damaged in combat. The vehicle's combination of cargo capacity and lifting power made it particularly valuable to independent FWLM regiments operating far from fixed repair facilities.","battleHistory":"The Free Worlds League commissioned the Paramour in the mid-twenty-sixth century as part of a broader effort to bring the FWLM up to Star League Defense Force standards. League planners recognized that matching the SLDF in firepower was impractical, and instead focused on logistical excellence as a way to demonstrate institutional competence. Gilbert, Sachs, and Belafonte, a conglomerate better known for building exotic excursion vehicles for wealthy Free Worlders, won the production contract. The company's experience with luxury vehicles translated into an unusually refined design for a military support platform.\\n\\nDuring the Star League era, duty aboard a Paramour became a prestigious assignment within the FWLM. Quartermasters routinely established their field headquarters aboard the vehicles, and daily inspections of crew areas and uniforms became standard practice. This attention to presentation drew scorn from visiting SLDF officers, who dismissed the Paramour crews as more concerned with appearance than function. The reputation proved unfair. When called upon to service combat units in the field, Paramour crews consistently delivered high-quality work that eventually won over even their harshest critics.\\n\\nGSB, Inc. did not survive the Second Succession War, and production of the Paramour ceased permanently. Although the vehicles were rigorously maintained wherever they served, attrition and entropy gradually thinned the ranks of functional examples over the following centuries.\\n\\nThe Jihad and the collapse of federal authority brought renewed attention to surviving Paramours. Two vehicles appeared on Regulus, where they were reconditioned for service with the Regulan Hussars. By replacing the quarters and cargo space with a mobile field base, the Hussars transformed the Paramour into a true repair vehicle suited to sustained independent operations. The Regulan Prince's intentions for deploying these rare vehicles remained a subject of speculation."}},"name":"Paramour Mobile Repair Vehicle","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":5,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":15,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":{"$k":"~7","$v":["~33",["lift-hoist","rear","#1",null,null],"~32","~34"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"16":{"tro":{"production":"~35","components":{"chassis":"Johnston Industries","engine":"Johnston Industries"},"text":{"overview":"The Prairie Schooner Land Train is a large wheeled support vehicle produced by Johnston Industries on New Syrtis, introduced in 3058. Johnston engineers drew on manufacturing data recovered from the Helm Memory Core to develop the design, including the techniques needed to build its fusion powerplant. Conceived for developing worlds lacking reliable rail or air networks, the ninety-ton tractor connects with up to three modular trailer units to form a complete land train capable of moving bulk cargo across hundreds of kilometers of undeveloped terrain. It quickly established itself as a commercial workhorse across the Capellan March, linking remote agricultural and mining communities to regional distribution centers. While not a military design, the Prairie Schooner's carrying capacity and cross-country mobility have occasionally drawn it into logistics support roles during wartime.","capabilities":"The Prairie Schooner tractor is built on a heavy wheeled large vehicle chassis with tractor and off-road modifications, enabling reliable mobility across the rough terrain typical of frontier worlds. Its fusion powerplant provides an effectively unlimited operational range, which is its most significant advantage over petrochemical competitors. The trade-off is cost: at roughly half a million C-bills for a complete land train, the Prairie Schooner is well outside the budget of smaller operators who can manage with less capable alternatives.\\n\\nThe tractor carries 17 tons of cargo in its main bay, accessible through a single rear door, and the bay serves as a warm hold by default for general freight. Its self-righting technology allows the vehicle to recover from rollovers in all but the most severe accidents, a feature that adds operational confidence on uneven ground. Operator quarters accommodate a second crew member for extended journeys without resupply stops.\\n\\nSix and a half tons of light composite armor provide protection against environmental hazards and minor threats common on undeveloped worlds, though the tractor carries no weapons from the factory. Customers on worlds with significant predator populations frequently add remotely operated light weapon blisters, typically armed with small arms or light support weapons. The Prairie Schooner is not designed to withstand military-grade weapons fire, and operators are advised to withdraw well before combat reaches their position.","deployment":"Prairie Schooner Land Train tractors operate across frontier and underdeveloped worlds throughout the Inner Sphere, with the heaviest concentrations in the Capellan March and adjacent regions. Civilian operations focus on bulk cargo transport between isolated settlements and regional hubs, with common payloads including grain, ore, manufactured goods, and construction materials.\\n\\nMilitary logistics units in multiple factions have conscripted or contracted Prairie Schooners for rear-echelon supply work, particularly in operational areas where road infrastructure is limited and refueling is impractical. Operators on hostile worlds routinely add light weapon systems to their tractors for defense against wildlife and low-level bandit threats.","battleHistory":"Johnston Industries began development of the Prairie Schooner in the mid-3050s, leveraging production data recovered from the Helm Memory Core by the New Avalon Institute of Science. Among the most valuable pieces of that recovered data were the manufacturing methods for fusion drive systems, which Johnston applied directly to the Prairie Schooner's powerplant. The design entered production at their New Syrtis facility in 3058, debuting as one of the more technically sophisticated civilian transport vehicles of the era.\\n\\nCommercial uptake was strong across underdeveloped worlds throughout the Capellan March. Agricultural communities valued the Prairie Schooner's ability to haul refrigerated and bulk cargo over long distances without concern for fuel supply, while resource extraction operations used standard cargo configurations to move ore from remote mine sites to processing centers. Johnston Industries marketed the system aggressively in the region, and the Prairie Schooner became closely associated with the March's frontier economy.\\n\\nThough civilian in purpose, the design attracted military attention almost immediately. The Word of Blake's 31st Division pressed Prairie Schooner trains into service on New Avalon at the outset of the Jihad, using them as part of their supply chain during their occupation of the Federated Suns capital. This was not an isolated incident. Commanders across multiple factions found that large land trains were practical logistics platforms when air transport was unavailable or at risk, and Prairie Schooners appeared in rear-echelon supply columns across several major campaigns.\\n\\nThe New Syrtis plant suffered severe damage during the Jihad. Taurian Concordat raids in 3076 struck the facility directly, and radiation contamination from those attacks rendered much of the production floor unusable for years afterward. Johnston Industries responded by upgrading their existing component facility on Benet III to a full production line, maintaining Prairie Schooner output throughout the disruption. The New Syrtis plant's recovery was slow, complicated by the broader devastation of the capital world and ongoing contamination cleanup efforts that extended well into the Republic era.\\n\\nProduction continued on Benet III through the Dark Age and into the ilClan period, with New Syrtis eventually returning to production as conditions stabilized. The design remained largely unchanged across its production run, its original specifications proving durable enough that major revisions were never pursued."}},"name":"Prairie Schooner Land Train","tonnage":90,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":17,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":34,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":30,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":30,"frontRight":30,"rearLeft":30,"rearRight":30,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":[["quarters-crew","#0"],["quarters-crew","#1"],["quarters-crew","#2"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"17":{"tro":{"production":"~35","components":{"chassis":"Johnston Industries"},"text":{"overview":"The Prairie Schooner Module is the standardized trailer component of the Johnston Industries Prairie Schooner Land Train system, designed to be towed by the Prairie Schooner tractor. Introduced alongside the tractor in 3058, each thirty-ton module connects to the tractor or to other modules in train sequences of up to three units. Modules are available in multiple configurations, including standard cargo, refrigerated, liquid container, passenger transport, and livestock variants. The Module's passive, interchangeable design is the foundation of the land train system's commercial versatility, multiplying hauling capacity without proportionally increasing operating costs beyond the investment in the tractor itself.","capabilities":"The Prairie Schooner Module uses a trailer chassis with off-road suspension and wide-profile tires that match the tractor's cross-country capability. The module has no independent propulsion, relying entirely on the tractor for movement. This simplicity makes it inexpensive to maintain and straightforward to repair in the field with basic tooling.\\n\\nEach standard module carries 22.5 tons of cargo in a main bay with two side access doors, one on the left and one on the right. This slightly larger capacity compared to the tractor's own bay, combined with the ability to chain three modules together, allows a fully loaded Prairie Schooner train to move well over 80 tons of goods in a single run. The standard configuration is a warm hold suited to general dry freight, though operators routinely specify refrigerated, liquid, passenger, or livestock configurations at the time of purchase.\\n\\nTwo and a half tons of light composite armor protect the module against the minor hazards of frontier operations. As with the tractor, the module carries no weapons from the factory. Customers on hostile worlds occasionally add turret blisters with light anti-infantry or area suppression weapons. The module's main operational limitation is its complete dependence on the tractor, as a disconnected module has no ability to reposition and becomes a fixed, vulnerable point.","deployment":"Prairie Schooner Modules operate wherever the tractor is found, predominantly on frontier and underdeveloped worlds throughout the Capellan March and beyond. Standard cargo modules are the most common configuration in day-to-day use. Refrigerated and liquid variants are especially prevalent on agricultural worlds where perishable goods supply chains are critical to local economies.\\n\\nPassenger modules serve colony transit and evacuation roles across isolated communities. In military logistics applications, modules carry ammunition, medical supplies, and general freight in rear-area support columns. On particularly hostile worlds, operators mount light weapons on module chassis for wildlife deterrence.","battleHistory":"The Prairie Schooner Module was developed simultaneously with the tractor as the essential companion component of Johnston Industries' land train concept. The design team prioritized interchangeability from the outset, ensuring each trailer would connect to any tractor and link in sequence with other modules without compatibility problems. This standardization was a deliberate commercial decision, intended to encourage operators to expand their fleets incrementally rather than commit to a single large purchase.\\n\\nProduction began at New Syrtis in 3058 alongside the tractor. The range of available configurations accelerated adoption across different markets. Refrigerated modules opened supply chain opportunities in the frontier food production sector, while passenger variants provided rural communities with a long-haul transport option that fixed-route vehicles could not deliver cost-effectively. Liquid cargo modules found ready buyers in fuel distribution and water transport roles on worlds with limited pipeline infrastructure.\\n\\nModule production followed the same disruption pattern as the tractor when the New Syrtis facility was damaged during the Jihad. Johnston's upgraded Benet III plant began producing modules alongside tractors during the recovery period. The module's simpler construction allowed Benet III to reach full module output relatively quickly, helping stabilize supply to operators during the years when the New Syrtis line was offline.\\n\\nThe variety of module types contributed to the Prairie Schooner system's long-term commercial durability. Some configurations were adapted for humanitarian logistics work during the Republic of the Sphere era, with large accessible cargo bays converted for medical supply distribution and emergency response roles. The module's unarmored cargo spaces could be reconfigured quickly with minimal tooling, a practical advantage in crisis situations where civilian equipment was pressed into aid distribution."}},"name":"Prairie Schooner Module","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":21.5,"doors":2}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":0,"armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":15,"right":15,"rear":12,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","trailer","off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"18":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Various","primaryFactory":"Various"},"text":{"overview":"The Randolph Support Vehicle is a primitive tracked utility design dating to circa 2300, one of countless logistical workhorses that sustained armies throughout the Age of War and Reunification War. Never an elite or specialized machine, its value lay in dependability: it kept combat forces supplied, maintained, and operational across a wide range of terrain and conditions. Manufactured by numerous concerns across the Inner Sphere, the Randolph was purchased in large quantities by national militaries, paramilitary organizations, and private military contractors alike. Its combination of modest armament, adequate cargo capacity, and rugged tracked chassis made it suitable for rear-echelon operations in the dangerous near-combat zone that support troops routinely occupied. Though declared obsolete by 2500, examples continued to serve in low-intensity rear-area roles for decades after that date.","capabilities":"The Randolph is a 35-ton vehicle powered by an ICE engine, providing a cruise speed of 40 km/h and a flank speed of 60 km/h. Its nine-ton enclosed cargo bay and rear-loading door are its primary assets. A tractor chassis modification allows the vehicle to tow trailers, sleds, or disabled vehicles, giving it effective logistics capacity well beyond its internal storage. The machine gun mounts use a universal design, allowing different light weapons to be fitted depending on the mission.\\n\\nFor self-defense, three machine guns cover the front, right flank, and left flank arcs. A Basic Fire Control system provides rudimentary targeting assistance. The vehicle's primitive-grade armor is well distributed across all facings, offering meaningful protection against small arms and fragmentation hazards common in rear-echelon environments.\\n\\nThe Randolph is straightforward to maintain, relying on widely available ICE components and a simple mechanical layout. The Modular Weapons configuration further simplifies field-level modification. Its ICE power plant generates no heat accumulation, appropriate given its non-energy weapon armament. The principal weaknesses are limited top speed, no electronic warfare capability, and vulnerability to any dedicated combat unit, which can bring firepower far exceeding what primitive armor can reliably absorb.","deployment":"The Randolph was deployed across the full spectrum of military logistics roles during the Age of War and Reunification War. It served with national armies of all major Inner Sphere powers, mercenary commands, and planetary defense forces. Typically assigned to supply battalions and logistics companies, it operated in cargo convoys, forward base construction teams, and mobile maintenance elements. Its towing capability made it particularly useful on long supply routes where cargo volume exceeded internal bay capacity. Field modifications as ambulances and command posts were documented across multiple theaters. By the mid-twenty-fifth century, the design was most commonly found in militia and reserve units on worlds that could not source more modern equipment.","battleHistory":"The Randolph emerged in the early twenty-fourth century as military planners across the Inner Sphere confronted a persistent operational problem: the support personnel and vehicles sustaining front-line combat units outnumbered those combat units by five-to-one or more, yet operated with little protection in environments only marginally safer than the front lines themselves. Vehicles like the Randolph were a direct response to that reality, offering armored enclosures and limited firepower to logistics crews who had no other means of self-defense.\\n\\nThe design saw wide adoption across the Inner Sphere during the Age of War. Its distributed manufacturing model, with regional armories and civilian producers building the type to meet local demand, meant that Randolphs appeared wherever armies operated. No single producer dominated the market. The design served throughout the Reunification War in the same roles that defined it, hauling artillery pieces, mobile repair equipment, and critical supplies across contested terrain. Some units were converted to improvised ambulances or field command posts, adapting the enclosed cargo bay to communications equipment.\\n\\nBy 2500, advancing technology made the Randolph's primitive construction economically unviable to upgrade. The Star League Defense Force required more capable logistics platforms, and production at most facilities wound down in favor of more modern designs. Surviving vehicles continued to serve in rear-area roles for organizations that could not afford newer equipment, particularly on colony worlds and in small mercenary units. A handful persisted into the early Succession Wars era on backwater systems, valued more for their simplicity and ease of maintenance than for any tactical capability, before finally passing out of service entirely."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter","equipmentUuid"],"$v":[["quirk-easy-to-maintain",null,null],["quirk-obsolete",2500,null],["quirk-modular-weapons",null,"#0"],["quirk-modular-weapons",null,"#1"],["quirk-modular-weapons",null,"#2"]]},"name":"Randolph Support Vehicle","tonnage":35,"hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":9,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":16,"right":16,"rear":14,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~7","$v":["~33",["machine-gun","right","#1",null,null],["machine-gun","left","#2",null,null],"~34"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":["tractor"],"bar":5,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"19":{"tro":{"production":"~5","components":{"engine":"ICE (rating unknown)","armor":"BAR 6 (3.5 tons)"},"text":{"overview":"The Reaper is a fifty-five-ton primitive self-propelled artillery vehicle introduced by the Terran Hegemony in 2321. Developed as the lighter counterpart to the heavier Apostle, the Reaper was designed to provide indirect fire support at the regimental and brigade level, filling a critical gap in the Hegemony Armed Forces' artillery capabilities. More mobile and numerous than its larger sibling, the Reaper traded raw firepower for flexibility and wider tactical deployment across the HAF's organizational structure. As one of the first generation of purpose-built Hegemony artillery platforms, the Reaper helped establish the doctrinal foundations for mobile fire support at the formation level.","capabilities":"The Reaper centers its firepower on a single Thumper artillery piece, complemented by three tons of ammunition providing sixty rounds of fire. An advanced fire control system with anti-aircraft targeting capability guides the weapon, giving the Reaper a secondary role as an impromptu air defense battery when circumstances demand. A front-mounted small laser serves as the vehicle's sole direct-fire defensive weapon.\\n\\nThe Reaper's internal combustion engine propels it at a cruise speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour with a flank speed of fifty-four, matching the Apostle in ground mobility. However, its single ton of fuel limits operational range to approximately 526 kilometers, considerably less than its heavier companion. Protection consists of 3.5 tons of BAR 6 armor, providing minimal coverage against anything beyond small arms and shrapnel. The vehicle suffers from poor environmental sealing, leaving the seven-member crew vulnerable to chemical, biological, or hazardous atmosphere conditions. Despite these limitations, the Reaper's lighter weight made it more practical for wider distribution across the HAF's regimental structure than the larger and more resource-intensive Apostle.","deployment":"The Reaper Self-Propelled Artillery served as the standard light artillery platform for Hegemony Armed Forces regiment and brigade-level formations. Its lighter weight and simpler logistics allowed wider distribution than its heavier companion, providing responsive indirect fire support closer to the front lines. The advanced fire control system's anti-aircraft targeting capability gave Reaper crews an additional air defense role, making the vehicle a versatile addition to formation-level artillery battalions.","battleHistory":"The Reaper originated alongside the Apostle as part of Field Marshal Mears Coblitz's comprehensive modernization of the Hegemony Armed Forces' artillery arm during the early years of the Terran Hegemony. While the Apostle addressed the need for heavy fire support at the division level, the Reaper filled the lighter requirement for regimental and brigade artillery battalions. Both vehicles moved from requirements to production remarkably fast, entering service in less than two years.\\n\\nThe Reaper's first operational deployment came during the later stages of the Campaign of Persuasion, where the HAF's newly modernized artillery corps proved its worth in supporting ground actions across multiple worlds. The lighter vehicle was particularly valued for its ability to be distributed more widely across the HAF's expanding force structure, providing organic fire support to formations that had previously relied on outdated Alliance-era equipment.\\n\\nOver the following decades, the Reaper and Apostle together displaced virtually all of the static, towed, and self-propelled artillery that the Hegemony had inherited from the Terran Alliance. Both platforms served for more than a century before the rapid pace of technological advancement overtook them. The Reaper was declared obsolete in 2480, slightly earlier than the Apostle, as newer designs employing what would become standard technology in later eras entered production. Few Reapers survived beyond their period of active service. By the thirty-first century, the remaining examples had been relegated to purely ceremonial roles, with a handful of relics enduring into the Dark Age as static displays on Terra and New Earth."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-poor-sealing",null],["quirk-obsolete",2480],["quirk-anti-aircraft-targeting",null]]},"name":"Reaper Self-Propelled Artillery","tonnage":55,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":{"front":18,"left":18,"right":18,"rear":18,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~6",["thumper-artillery","front","#1",null],["ammo-thumper-standard",null,"#2","thumper-artillery"],["ammo-thumper-standard",null,"#3","thumper-artillery"],["ammo-thumper-standard",null,"#4","thumper-artillery"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"advanced"}},"20":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Veridian Dynamics","primaryFactory":"Ozawa"},"text":{"overview":"The RR-3 Recovery Vehicle was developed by Veridian Dynamics on Ozawa as a commercial utility platform designed from the outset for battlefield recovery operations. Where the Terran Hegemony had invested in a purpose-built heavy design tied directly to the Mackie program, Veridian Dynamics pursued a lighter, more economical alternative suited to recovering disabled BattleMechs up to the heavy class. The RR-3 was the culmination of several earlier Veridian recovery vehicles, distilling the practical lessons of those designs into a single capable platform. Its wheeled configuration and internal combustion powerplant kept production costs accessible, allowing wide distribution to formations that could not obtain Hegemony-restricted assets. Though a primitive design by later standards, the RR-3 established many of the core operating principles that would guide battlefield recovery vehicle development for generations to come.","capabilities":"The RR-3 is a fifty-five-ton wheeled medium vehicle powered by an internal combustion engine delivering a cruising speed of 30 km/h and a flank speed of 50 km/h. Off-Road and Tractor Chassis Modifications allow it to operate across broken terrain and to tow disabled vehicles through conditions that would defeat a simpler platform, making it viable in the forward combat zones where recovery work most often occurs. Two lift hoists mounted at the rear provide the vehicle's primary function, each rated for six-ton loads.\\n\\nThe cargo deck holds twenty tons of standard freight and can be configured to carry a secured BattleMech hull. The RR-3 carries no weapons and relies entirely on any escorting security element for protection in contested areas. Four tons of BAR 6-rated hull armor deflect light shrapnel and small arms fire but offer little resistance to dedicated anti-vehicle weapons. The Gas Hog quirk means fuel consumption is higher than the vehicle's modest speed would suggest, and the Fragile Fuel Tank requires careful attention in any combat-adjacent environment. Offsetting these drawbacks, the Easy to Maintain rating allows field technicians to service the vehicle with basic tools and common spare parts, a significant advantage for a platform expected to operate far from major depots.","deployment":"The RR-3 was distributed to unit-level logistics and support formations throughout the Terran Hegemony and early Great House militaries during the Age of War and the early Star League period. Its off-road capability and towing capacity suited it to a wide variety of terrain types, and the ICE powerplant allowed operation where sophisticated fuel infrastructure was unavailable. The design's weight class was compatible with standard DropShip loadouts common to combined-arms regiments, easing routine deployment alongside the BattleMechs it supported. Second-line and garrison formations continued operating the type past its official obsolescence date in 2550, often partnering with newer recovery vehicles when the older design's cargo capacity remained useful.","battleHistory":"The first recovery vehicles to appear on BattleMech-era battlefields were improvised affairs, standard construction cranes and heavy-transport winches adapted by logistics crews struggling to keep pace with the massive new combat machines transforming warfare across the Inner Sphere. Veridian Dynamics recognized the commercial opportunity early, developing a series of progressively more capable dedicated recovery platforms through the 2430s. Each iteration addressed shortcomings of the last, improving off-road performance, load capacity, and crew ergonomics as the operational picture became clearer.\\n\\nThe RR-3 entered production at the Veridian Dynamics facility on Ozawa in 2439, alongside the Hegemony's UTR-588, as a lighter and more economical alternative suited to unit-level recovery rather than depot-scale salvage operations. Its wheeled platform and ICE powerplant made it affordable and maintainable for formations throughout the Terran Hegemony and the surrounding realms. The design filled a clear gap, providing formations that lacked access to the Hegemony's classified heavy assets with a capable tool for keeping their BattleMechs in the fight.\\n\\nThe RR-3 served widely through the Age of War and into the early Star League era, becoming a familiar piece of equipment in the logistics trains of combined-arms regiments across the Hegemony and the Great Houses alike. Its ease of maintenance and low operating cost kept it popular with units operating far from major supply depots. The design's limitations became unavoidable as BattleMechs grew heavier and more complex, and by 2550 the RR-3 was officially declared obsolete as newer purpose-built recovery vehicles with greater lifting capacity entered service. Production wound down gradually as replacement designs accumulated in the supply chain, and surviving hulls were reassigned to second-line logistics roles before final retirement."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~13",["quirk-fragile-fuel-tank",null],["quirk-obsolete",2550],["quirk-gas-hog",null]]},"name":"RR-3 Recovery Vehicle","tonnage":55,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":20,"doors":1}],"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":15,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~36","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1.5,"chassisModifications":["tractor","off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"21":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grand Union Battle Works","primaryFactory":"Tikonov"},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Strike Falcon Attack VTOL was developed by Grand Union Battle Works on Tikonov, entering service in 2371 as the Capellan Confederation's answer to the aging OT-22 Bird of Prey rotary-wing platform. Designed to fill a demanding dual role, the Strike Falcon combined heavy infantry transport capacity with substantial fire support capability, making it one of the more versatile VTOL designs of the Age of War. Its introduction was enthusiastically received by Capellan commanders, who found its combination of speed, range, and cargo capacity well suited to the mobile warfare of the era.\\n\\nThe design's reputation spread quickly beyond Capellan borders, and multiple powers attempted to replicate its performance with varying degrees of success. Despite its eventual obsolescence, the Strike Falcon remained in frontline service for over a century before giving way to lighter, faster, and better-protected successors.","capabilities":"The Strike Falcon is a 30-ton medium VTOL built around an internal combustion engine, delivering a cruise speed of approximately 119 km/h and a flank speed of 186 km/h. Its primary offensive systems are a pair of SRM 4 launchers mounted in the nose, providing effective short-range fire against armored targets. Three machine guns, positioned in the chin and on both flanks, offer broad-arc anti-infantry suppression. An Advanced Fire Control system improves crew accuracy, partially compensating for the vehicle's relatively modest armament compared to heavier dedicated gunships.\\n\\nThe aircraft's most operationally significant feature is its large troop bay, designed to carry one fully equipped heavy infantry platoon, or two light platoons in cramped conditions, through three access doors on the right, left, and rear. The wings serve a secondary aerodynamic function, contributing significant lift in forward flight and extending the operational radius to well over 1,500 kilometers. BAR 7 armor provides adequate protection against small arms and light weapons common to the period, but leaves the vehicle dangerously exposed to any concentrated anti-armor fire. The Easy to Pilot design quirk made the Strike Falcon accessible to a broad pool of trained crews, an important advantage when qualified rotary-wing pilots were in short supply across the Confederation's expanding military.","deployment":"The Strike Falcon served across Capellan Confederation ground forces as an assault transport and fire support asset throughout the Age of War. Commanders typically deployed pairs or flights of three to insert a company-sized infantry element, with the aircraft transitioning to suppression and covering fire after troops cleared the landing zone. The design saw action along multiple contested borders, operating in coordination with ground mechanized units to exploit breakthrough opportunities and secure objectives ahead of slower-moving armor.\\n\\nAs the design aged into the mid-twenty-fourth century, Strike Falcons increasingly appeared in reserve and militia formations, replaced in frontline roles by more capable platforms. Following the 2510 obsolescence declaration, units transferred to civilian operators retained their transport capacity but were stripped of weapons and combat sensors.","battleHistory":"The helicopter's place on the battlefield was never seriously in doubt during the Age of War. When the Capellan Confederation coalesced from a collection of smaller states in 2367, Tikonov contributed not just territory but a robust defense industrial base centered on Grand Union Battle Works. The OT-22 Bird of Prey had been the backbone of Tikonov's rotary-wing forces, but it was approaching the end of its service life. Grand Union Battle Works received the commission to develop a capable successor that could meet the Confederation's demands for combined-arms flexibility.\\n\\nThe Strike Falcon entered service in 2371 and quickly earned a strong operational reputation in Capellan formations. Its ability to insert infantry directly into contested areas, then remain on station to provide immediate fire support, made it a preferred tool for battalion and regiment commanders throughout the Age of War. Few opponents could take one down with a single hit in its heyday, and the design's durability drew attention across the Inner Sphere. Several rival powers and independent manufacturers produced copies or near-copies of the Strike Falcon during the mid-twenty-fourth century, though results were mixed.\\n\\nThe aircraft's shortcomings grew more pronounced as the era progressed. Advances in weapons and armor technology increasingly outpaced the Strike Falcon's protection levels, and newer platforms offered better performance across every mission parameter. By 2510, Grand Union Battle Works officially declared the design obsolete. Surviving airframes were stripped of their combat systems and transitioned to civilian cargo and passenger roles, where some remained in operation for decades after military retirement.\\n\\nThe Grand Union Battle Works facility on Tikonov continued operating through subsequent centuries under shifting ownership. Tikonov's contested position in Inner Sphere politics brought the world through Capellan Confederation, Tikonov Free Republic, and Federated Commonwealth control during the Succession Wars and Clan Invasion eras, before returning to Capellan hands during the Civil War period. The planet later passed through independent administration, Republic of the Sphere stewardship, a brief Federated Suns interval, and ultimately back to the Confederation during the ilClan era. The factory itself, long since repurposed from Strike Falcon production, reflects that turbulent history of planetary control."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-easy-to-pilot",null],["quirk-obsolete",2510]]},"name":"Strike Falcon Attack VTOL","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":6,"cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":{"front":10,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":5,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","location","uuid","feedsWeaponTag","halfTon"],"$v":[["srm-4","front","#0",null,null],["srm-4","front","#1",null,null],["machine-gun","front","#2",null,null],["machine-gun","right","#3",null,null],["machine-gun","left","#4",null,null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#5","srm-4",null],["~8",null,"#6","machine-gun",true]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"22":{"tro":{"production":"~37","components":"~38","text":{"overview":"The Sturmvogel Gunship is the original military prototype that preceded the more widely known Maritime Patrol variant. Developed by Nashan Diversified on Furillo in 3059, the design emerged from an attempt to build a Lyran combat WiGE with meaningful offensive reach. Though rejected by Lyran Alliance Armed Forces evaluators for insufficient armor protection, the Gunship established the speed, power plant, and modular foundation that the Maritime Patrol design built upon. Small numbers entered service with Lyran militia and planetary defense forces who valued its firepower over its survivability, and the design has remained available from Nashan's Furillo facility despite never achieving the widespread adoption of its better-known sibling.","capabilities":"The Sturmvogel Gunship shares its power plant with the Maritime Patrol variant, reaching a maximum speed above 120 kilometers per hour with an unlimited patrol range. The primary difference lies in its armament. Rather than the rotary autocannon of the standard design, the Gunship mounts a light Gauss rifle, providing greater reach and accuracy at the cost of the burst-fire volume that makes the Maritime Patrol effective at close engagement ranges. Three mine dispensers remain for area-denial, though one fewer than the standard model reduces flexibility when laying barrier fields.\\n\\nThe Gunship's most pronounced weakness is its armor, which falls short even of the Maritime Patrol's already thin protection. This deficiency was the central reason LAAF evaluators rejected the design for front-line combat service. Crews compensate through the same high-speed, terrain-exploiting tactics used by Maritime Patrol crews, keeping the craft at stand-off range and avoiding direct engagement with armored opponents. For operators willing to accept the survivability trade-off, the light Gauss rifle provides a genuine long-range capability that the rotary autocannon cannot duplicate, giving the Gunship a distinct role when used in conjunction with better-protected patrol craft.","deployment":"The Sturmvogel Gunship is deployed as a fire support and interdiction asset in units lacking access to heavier WiGE combat vehicles. The light Gauss rifle gives it a stand-off advantage over the standard Maritime Patrol model, making it useful for harassing incoming waterborne forces and supporting other patrol craft from a distance. Mine dispensers provide secondary area-denial capability. Commanders typically hold the Gunship behind the engagement line, using faster and better-armed surface assets to screen it while it engages at range. Independent operations against armored opposition without support are strongly discouraged given the design's vulnerability to return fire.","battleHistory":"Nashan Diversified began development of what would become the Sturmvogel line in the late 3050s, following the Lyran Alliance's secession from the Federated Commonwealth in 3058. With access to Federated Suns-built patrol WiGEs growing uncertain, Nashan's designers set out to produce a Lyran-built replacement with genuine combat capability. The company's Furillo facility, already experienced in vehicle construction, was tasked with delivering a WiGE gunship that could compete with existing patrol craft in both speed and firepower.\\n\\nThe resulting prototype, completed in 3059, carried a light Gauss rifle and mine dispensers on a high-speed WiGE hull. LAAF evaluators were impressed by the weapon fit but concluded the armor protection was inadequate for any role involving direct engagement with enemy combat vehicles. The rejection pushed Nashan to pivot the design toward coast guard and militia markets, producing the Maritime Patrol variant with a rotary autocannon and search-and-rescue equipment in its place. The Maritime Patrol eclipsed the Gunship commercially, but Nashan continued to offer the Gunship configuration for customers who prioritized stand-off firepower over versatility.\\n\\nThe Gunship found a modest market among militia commanders defending contested coastlines who needed reach that the standard model's rotary autocannon could not provide. Production at Furillo continued alongside the Maritime Patrol line through the Civil War, the Jihad, and into the Dark Age, though the Gunship always remained the rarer of the two designs."}},"name":"Sturmvogel Gunship","model":"WiGE","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~39","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["gauss-rifle-light","front","#0",null],"~40","~41","~42",["~43",null,"#4","gauss-rifle-light"],["~43",null,"#5","gauss-rifle-light"],["~43",null,"#6","gauss-rifle-light"]]},"supportVehicle":"~44"},"23":{"tro":{"production":"~37","components":"~38","text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The standard Sturmvogel is deployed as a coastal patrol and fast interception craft, most commonly with coast guard units and planetary militias. Crews use the mine dispensers to pre-deploy barrier fields in predictable pirate lanes, or release mines directly in a pursuer's path when being chased. The remote sensor dispenser gives the craft a strong search and rescue capability, and the cargo bay is ordinarily stocked with rescue equipment rather than weapon stores. The craft operates equally well over open ocean, coastal wetlands, and frozen tundra, making it effective wherever conventional watercraft cannot follow.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"~48","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":3.5,"doors":0}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~39","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["rotary-ac-2","front","#0",null],"~40","~41","~42","~49",["~29","rear","#5",null],["~50",null,"#6","rotary-ac-2"],["~50",null,"#7","rotary-ac-2"],["~50",null,"#8","rotary-ac-2"]]},"supportVehicle":"~44"},"24":{"tro":{"production":"~37","components":"~38","text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The LB-10X represents the most heavily armed Sturmvogel variant. Removing the remote sensor dispenser, the cargo hold, and one mine dispenser freed enough tonnage to mount an LB 10-X autocannon in place of the lighter rotary weapon. The result is a patrol craft capable of seriously threatening even medium combat vehicles at range. This variant sacrifices nearly all of the versatility that defines the base design, making it poorly suited for rescue or interdiction work but valuable where pure firepower is the priority. It is most often found with front-line militia units facing credible armored threats along contested coastlines.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"~48","model":"(LB-10X)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~39","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["autocannon-lbx-10","front","#0",null],"~40","~41","~42",["~51",null,"#4","autocannon-lbx-10"],["~51",null,"#5","autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster",null,"#6","autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster",null,"#7","autocannon-lbx-10"]]},"supportVehicle":"~44"},"25":{"tro":{"production":"~37","components":"~38","text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The LB-2X refit trades the rotary autocannon and remote sensor dispenser for an LB 2-X autocannon, prioritizing accuracy and engagement range over burst-fire capability. This variant is more common in paramilitary and light military service where precise engagement at longer distances matters more than mine-laying support. Without the remote sensor dispenser, the LB-2X is less suited to search and rescue work, limiting its appeal for pure coast guard operations. Units that need a patrol craft with harder reach at range tend to prefer it over the standard model.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"~48","model":"(LB-2X)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":6,"doors":0}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~39","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["autocannon-lbx-2","front","#0",null],"~40","~41","~42","~49",["ammo-is-lb-2x-standard",null,"#5","autocannon-lbx-2"],["ammo-is-lb-2x-standard",null,"#6","autocannon-lbx-2"],["ammo-is-lb-2x-cluster",null,"#7","autocannon-lbx-2"]]},"supportVehicle":"~44"},"26":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Hegemony Research and Development Department (Weapons Division)","primaryFactory":"New Earth"},"text":{"overview":"The UTR-588 Recovery Vehicle was one of the earliest vehicles designed from the ground up for BattleMech recovery, developed by the Terran Hegemony's Research and Development Department at New Earth in direct parallel with the Mackie program itself. Where earlier recovery equipment had been adapted from commercial construction machinery, the UTR-588 was engineered specifically to handle the hundred-ton assault machines the Hegemony was bringing to the battlefield. Its ninety-ton tracked hull, fusion powerplant, and purpose-built cargo bed represented a significant investment in the support infrastructure required to keep BattleMechs operational under field conditions. The design served as a template for the category of purpose-built BattleMech recovery vehicles that would become a fixture of every major military's logistical apparatus for generations.","capabilities":"The UTR-588 is a ninety-ton tracked vehicle powered by a fusion engine, giving it the sustained power output and raw mass necessary to handle the largest BattleMechs of its era. Nominal cruise speed is 40 km/h and flank speed 60 km/h, though the Poor Performance design quirk means actual operational speed falls below those figures in practice, a characteristic attributed to primitive powerplant calibration tolerances. Ten heat sinks manage the fusion plant's waste heat, an early engineering lesson in the thermal demands of fusion-powered ground vehicles.\\n\\nHeavy tracked suspension provides the stability needed to drag disabled BattleMechs from swamps, rubble, and other difficult terrain, and the vehicle's own mass serves as counter-balance during recovery operations. Two rear-mounted lift hoists, each rated for six tons, can recover both BattleMechs and disabled vehicles. The forty-ton cargo bed features purpose-built mounting points and restraint hardware sized for a strapped-down BattleMech hull. The UTR-588 carries no weapons and is entirely reliant on escort for protection. Seven tons of BAR 6-rated armor provide reasonable resistance against incidental battle damage. The Obsolete (2530) quirk reflects the rapid advancement of recovery vehicle technology that followed the BattleMech's proliferation, as each realm developed its own more capable analogs.","deployment":"The UTR-588 was deployed exclusively with Terran Hegemony heavy assault formations during the Age of War, assigned at the regiment or battalion level to accompany assault-class BattleMech units. Its tracked configuration and heavy mass suited it to difficult terrain operations where wheeled recovery vehicles could not function effectively. The design was not exported to the Great House militaries, remaining a Hegemony-specific asset throughout its operational service. As SLDF formations absorbed surviving Hegemony assets following the Star League's founding, some UTR-588 hulls entered service with SLDF depot and training commands before the type was fully superseded by more capable recovery vehicles.","battleHistory":"The development of the UTR-588 was inseparable from the development of the Mackie. As Hegemony engineers worked on the first true BattleMech at the Weapons Division facility on New Earth, the logistical support staff recognized that no existing recovery vehicle was capable of pulling a hundred-ton combat machine out of the field conditions where it would inevitably be disabled. The decision to develop a dedicated recovery platform in parallel with the Mackie itself reflected the Hegemony's systematic approach to warfare, building the support infrastructure alongside the primary weapon system from the beginning rather than improvising solutions after the fact.\\n\\nThe UTR-588 entered service in 2439 alongside the first Mackie production examples and proved its value almost immediately. The BattleMech's combination of extreme mass and the difficult terrain it was designed to operate in made recovery operations a routine part of every major engagement. The UTR-588's tracks, counter-balancing weight, and dedicated mounting hardware made it far more effective than the improvised solutions that other realms were still relying upon, and the design gained a reputation as an essential companion to any assault formation deploying the new Mackies.\\n\\nThe rapid proliferation of BattleMechs through the Age of War created demand for newer and more capable recovery designs, and by 2530 the UTR-588 was declared obsolete as follow-on vehicles with improved performance entered production. Surviving hulls were redistributed to training commands and second-line depot roles, where many remained in service for years after the design officially left the front line. The New Earth facility continued producing spare parts until production was fully wound down. The UTR-588's legacy was the concept it proved, that a purpose-built recovery vehicle was an essential component of any force built around assault-class BattleMechs, a lesson that every major military in the Inner Sphere would eventually internalize."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-obsolete",2530],"~52"]},"name":"UTR-588 Recovery Vehicle","tonnage":90,"hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":40,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":50,"left":50,"right":50,"rear":34,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~36","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"27":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Bigoti Seacraft","primaryFactory":"Keid","targeting":"Advanced Fire Control"},"components":{"chassis":"Bigoti Seacraft"},"text":{"overview":"The White Tip command submarine was developed by Bigoti Seacraft on Keid in 3074, serving the Word of Blake Protectorate as a mobile command platform for aquatic operations. Rather than building to standard military specifications, Bigoti Seacraft drew on its experience in the civilian shipbuilding market, producing a vessel that met uprated civilian standards at a fraction of the cost of a true military design. This practical approach delivered a platform that could function both as a mobile headquarters and as an auxiliary combatant for coastal patrol work. The White Tip relied on its submersible capability to evade hostile forces rather than on heavy armor protection, a design philosophy that made it well-suited for contested aquatic environments. Despite its modest origins, the design proved resilient enough to outlast its original patron and eventually attract commercial interest well beyond the Protectorate.","capabilities":"The White Tip is a 250-ton naval vessel designed around dual roles as a command platform and coastal combat asset. Its primary offensive capability comes from two Long Range Torpedo-15 launchers, one positioned forward and one aft, giving the vessel simultaneous coverage across both arcs. A Long Range Torpedo-10 rounds out the long-range battery, mounted in the turret alongside two Short Range Torpedo-4 launchers for close-range defense. An Extended Range Medium Laser in the bow provides a directed-energy option effective against surface targets and watercraft that may be resistant to torpedo fire.\\n\\nThe White Tip's armor is minimal by any military standard, relying on just four tons of civilian-grade material rated to BAR 8. This thin protection reflects the core design philosophy of avoidance over absorption. The vessel is intended to remain submerged and difficult to locate, not to absorb fire from dedicated combat units. A cruise speed of 3 and flank speed of 5 are unimpressive for its displacement, but underwater concealment compensates for what it lacks in straight-line performance.\\n\\nCommand facilities are substantial relative to the vessel's tonnage. Seven tons of communications equipment support a wide-spectrum communications capability, and the ship carries a MASH unit, medical quarters, and berthing for officers and support personnel. Advanced Fire Control guides the weapons suite. A 30-ton cargo hold allows extended independent deployment, reducing reliance on shore support during prolonged operations.","deployment":"The White Tip is deployed on worlds with significant aquatic terrain, where its submersible capability provides both a tactical and operational advantage. Operating as a mobile command platform, it is best suited to coordinating coastal and riverine operations from submerged positions that deny enemy forces an easy targeting solution. Long-range torpedo batteries allow engagement of surface and underwater targets at standoff distances, while the command facilities give embarked commanders the communications reach to direct dispersed forces across wide areas. The design's light protection means White Tip commanders favor submerged standoff tactics over surface engagements. Medical and personnel support facilities make extended independent deployments practical, allowing the vessel to serve as a self-sufficient mobile headquarters during prolonged coastal operations.","battleHistory":"Bigoti Seacraft was a Keid-based company with deep roots in civilian maritime construction when the Word of Blake commissioned the White Tip in 3074. The Protectorate sought an affordable command platform for aquatic environments, and Bigoti's expertise in commercial vessel construction made them a natural choice. Rather than demanding military-specification systems throughout, the Blakists accepted a design built to enhanced civilian standards. This kept unit costs manageable while delivering the capabilities the Protectorate required: submersible operations, torpedo armament, and extensive command and communications facilities.\\n\\nWhen Devlin Stone's Coalition forces arrived to liberate Keid, all six operational White Tips were deployed against the invaders. The submarines proved capable coastal defenders, using their submersible nature to strike and withdraw before Coalition forces could effectively respond. Despite the Protectorate's ultimate defeat, the performance of the White Tips was noted by Coalition commanders. The vessels demonstrated real utility as mobile command posts and coastal patrol assets, and Stone's forces committed resources to recovering and rebuilding four of the six submarines. The two destroyed in combat were beyond recovery.\\n\\nThe surviving White Tips were pressed into Coalition service, effectively transferring the design's operational legacy from the Word of Blake to what would become the Republic of the Sphere. Bigoti Seacraft, now operating under Republic governance on Keid, formalized this transition by beginning to market the White Tip to off-world customers by 3079. The design's low unit cost and proven submersible capability made it an attractive option for planetary militias and corporate security forces on aquatically active worlds.\\n\\nIn the ilClan era, Keid's ownership passed to the Capellan Confederation, bringing Bigoti Seacraft's production line under Capellan authority."}},"name":"White Tip Submarine","tonnage":250,"motiveType":"naval-sub","bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":30,"doors":1}],"era":"jihad","armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":14,"right":14,"rear":17,"turret":20,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","location","feedsWeaponTag"],"$v":[["~28","#0",null,null],["field-kitchen","#1",null,null],["mash-core","#2",null,null],["srt-4","#3","front",null],["lrt-15","#4","front",null],["lrt-15","#5","rear",null],["srt-4","#6","turret",null],["lrt-10","#7","turret",null],["er-medium-laser","#8","turret",null],["ammo-srt-standard","#9",null,"srt-4"],["ammo-srt-standard","#10",null,"srt-4"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#11",null,"lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#12",null,"lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#13",null,"lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#14",null,"lrt-10"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#15",null,"lrt-10"],["quarters-officer","#16",null,null],["quarters-officer","#17",null,null],["quarters-officer","#18",null,null],["quarters-crew","#19",null,null],["quarters-crew","#20",null,null],["quarters-crew","#21",null,null],["quarters-crew","#22",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#23",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#24",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#25",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#26",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#27",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#28",null,null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["submersible"],"bar":8,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"advanced"}}}`)};export{e as default};